        <p>TO THE EDITOR OF THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER. Bra,-</p>
        <p>As the pablie mind Is just now consider-oply dis- turbed, and very naturally so, by what is known as "The Beer Scare," we Ithink it may allay anxiety to some extent if we state that the Beers brewed in our Breweries (Wilderspool and St. Helens) are, mil always have been, absolutely pure and wholesome, and entirely free from arsenic and other deleterious matter. For Greenall, Whitley &amp; Co., Ltd., THUS J. DOWN. Director and Secretary: Wilderspool Brewery,• Warrington, 29th November, 19:)0. Municipal Offices, Warrington, lit December, Ina</p>
        <p>To Messrs. Greensll, Whitley, a: Co., Ltd., Warrington.</p>
        <p>DEAR SIRS,— / ana pleased to inform you that the samples of Beer taken from the " Blackburn Arms," " Ilaw. thorn Inn," and " Old Ball, ' are free from Arsenio.</p>
        <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>JOHN li: OORNALL.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>3tr DIL&amp;R VILLIS,</p>
        <p>I saw yesterday a black cloth walking dress._ with shaped flounce in cut-out cloth, over dark grey. Whe vest matched this, the rest of the bodice being in the cloth. The strappings on both skirt and bodice were in dull black silk. The sleeves were in the new shape, fitting closely to the arm, and widening out at the wrists over black silk zuadersleeves. A stitched tab of the served _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>1.0 hold the two sides of the cloth bodice together. file muff the lady was carrying was made of black velvet, silk, and frills of the cut-out cloth. The bat was composed of the same materials as the muff.</p>
        <p>When</p>
        <p>grey , the effect of mourning, especially worn, as is usually the case, with a black hat, black tie, and tdsek gloves. These tailormades are far more msrviceable than black, which stains so disagree- ably with mud. Already purple dresses are seen in some numbers, and it seems probable that the mish of the King that purple should take the place of black in house-draping may serve to introduce this rich colour as orthodox wear in mourning. Purple cloth with black braid, or trimmed with stitchings and strappings of black silk, and worn with a hat of black tucked silk, would even now be sufficiently expressive of general mourning. The only thing to remember is that with grey or black or white dresses all the accompaniments should be unrelieved black.</p>
        <p>I think that you will recognise that a graceful flare at the bottom is an essential feature of the accentuated by the circular flounces. Blue satin- .sewest skirts, and in many a mode this is faced cloth, with machine-stitching for the only ornamentation, was chosen for the skirt, which consists of five gores and is distinguished by .a ripple habit back. Hip darts secure the smooth adjustment at the top, where the conservative or decided dip is given; and the closing is made at the left side front seam. Either one or two flounces may be used, but the skirt is equally modish without them and at the lower edge, it is fashioned for a sweep or round length, and mea- sures about four yards and one-half in the medium a:zer,</p>
        <p>When you have a nice face cloth for your jacket it should be made quite short, and single- breasted, to allow of its being left open in the warm weather. Ido not quite like the idea of a grey straw hat on which to mount natural ostrich feathers. A soft beaver felt, almost the same colour as the feathers, would be much softer and more becoming. Lay the plumes fairly flat, and fasten in the centre with a paste buckle. A picture hatinvariably looks well in black, and one may be made with a double brim, the topsuost one being covered in chiffon and the lower one in tucked glace silk. A jet buckle is added with a feather drooping over the left ear, and this smart style is finished with chiffon rosettes on each side. Nothing is more necessary for mourning wear than the hat, which at all times is the finish to a stylish attire. If you are fond of pretty needlework you might make a very pretty valance for a bedroom mantel- shelf, with graceful bouquets of pink poppies and pale-green folia.ge applied to a ground of soft tur- quoise linen, the bunches being connected by love. knots of sarkenet ribbon of a slightly deeper blue neatly sewn down to the linen. The flowers and leaves might be touched up effectively with a little stitchery in shades of green and pink, and the en- tire design, including the ribbons, was outlined with Japanese gold thread. The valance can be finished at its edge with a band of narrow gold galon, fastened down with herring-boning worked in turquoise silk, and the result was a very hand- some drapery, althorugh, as the materials were inex- pensive and odds.2.nd ends of silk and gold thread were made use of,lhe cost was exceedingly smalL An uncommon variety of applique, again, has its origin in a beautiful old bed-spread of the time of Charles IL The ground material can be white satin, satin-sheeting, or good quality sateen, but for the applique fine white linen must be used, the design being outlined with thin gold-cord, and the shading put in with fine black silk. Perhaps I may turn from mourning and refer to wedding garments. A wedding dress which I have seen was of silver cloth, worked with massive embroidery in real silver, the design being caught up with silver-woven garlaads of orange blossom. The skirt opens in front over a plisse of white mousseline, embroidered at the hem with silver, and looped with trails of orange blossoms. The long train forms scallops at the edges, bordered With bouillonnees of white mousseline. The mantean de cour was in white velours, falling in deep, fun folds bordered with sables and lined with ermine. Amongst the many toilettes is one of mauve satin, incrusted with white Chantilly lace, and veiled with painted mousseline. Your loving, Jamul.</p>
