      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>'HE SALE CA</head>
        <p>'HE SALE CA</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Lovedf change has never been a charael eristic of Queen Alexandra, and it is therefore scarcely surprising to learn that she feels keenly leaving her Lsnclon hemp. Marlborough House, for Buckingham Palace. In one respect however the Queen will distinctly gain, and that is in the matter of tbe gardens, those of Buckingham Palace being far aad away the more beautiful. Sur- rounded by London heuses no one would suppose that they actually exceed forty acres in extent, and contain , a fairly large lake, with its swans and waterfowl, as secluded in appearance as any such scene might be fifty miles from the metropolis. It was here that James I. planted his famous Mulberry Gardens for the cultiva- tion of silkworms, but at that time there was hadly a house between the village of Kensington, and what Is now central London. v Princess Victoria has happily quite reit:oYMa frfim- her recent indisposition, which unfortunately prevented her taking any part in the recent opening nf Parliament As a younger mem.ber of the Prince of Wales' family. Her Royal flightless has hitherto lived in comparative .retirement, but as the only unmarried daughter of the King, the Princess will be brought into much greater prominence. Once more there are rumours to the effect that Her Royal Highness's engagement to Prince George Of Greece cull shortly be announced. Prince George it will be remembered, is a first cousin of Princess Victoria. and moreover, one of the handsomest Princes in Europe. Many biographies have appeared of Queen Victoria," at more than ordinary interest will attach to the Duke of Argyll's "Life of the Queen," which is to appear lad a popular edition of sixpenny fortnightly parts. It ws hoped ttiat Sir Theodore Martin might be able to under- take the task of writing an authoritative biography of her late Majesty. As the biographer of Prince Con- sort he had access to many private papers, and both he and his wife, the ;late Helen Faucit, were the intimate and trusted friends of the Queen. Sir Theodore is, however' in his eighty-fifth year, and it this age he does not care to enter upon so gigantic a task. There remains however, the Duke of Argyll, who, as a son in law of the late Queen, will enjoy many advan- tages which otter writers cannot possess. Ills long and intimate acquaintance with the Court, combined with his proved literary talents, should give us by far the Most interesting "Life of the Queen" that has, as yet, appeared. In this work the writer will have the invalu- able assistance of the Duchess, who is, unquestionable, the most clever, as well as the handsomest of all the late Queen's daughters. Her Royal Highness is the King's favourite sister, and is as charming as she is•gracious and unaffected. The publication of the life of Archbishop Benson 11 particularly timely in showing that the Queen was en. tirely in accord with Dr Benson in the desire to promote the greater charity and peace which has distinguished the religious life of the people during the past few years. On the subject of the selection for bishoprics we find her Majesty writing to the Archbishop in 1888 that the men to be taken muet be chosen for their real worth, and adding, "We want large, broad views, or the diffi- culties will become insurmountable." One sentence from the diary of the Archbishop is somewhat significant of the present troubles in the Church of England, where he expresses a doubt as to whether he ought to wear a decoration which had been given to him by the Queen, land adds :—" But obedience is the want of our time." It is given to few women to marry their dauehters as brilliantly as Mrs Cornwallis West has done. Nine years ago, when but eighteen, Miss Daisy Cornwallis West be- Cisme Princess Henry of Please, and now Miss Shelagh lilfest will in future be known as the Duchess of West- lniinster. 'lwo more beautiful women it would be dill. cult to find. The new Duchess with her dark eyes and hair, and rich colouring,repreduces much of the brilliant luxurious beauty of her ever-charming mcther, while the Princess possesses an exquisitely fair complexion, blue eyes, and sunny-tinted hair. - With members of our own Royal Family, Princese Henry is a great favourite,and ths Kaiser's attitude towards her has ever been cordial in the extreme. Much of her time is, of course, spent in Germany, but she generally manages to come over for the Lmdon season.</p>
        <p>I blue</p>
        <p>The new Duchess of Westminster adds yet another to the many instances in which the title of " Miss " has I been changed for that of Duchess. The Duchess of Port- land, it will be remembered, was a Miss Dallas Yorke ; the Duchess of Bedford a Mise Tribe ; trod the Duchess of Marlborough, as everyone knows, was Miss Consuelo Vaederbilt. The Duchess of Newcastle too, was a Miss Candy ; the Duchess of Wellington, a Miss Williams ; while the Duke of Macchester's romantic marriage with Miss Zimmerman was a recent sensation. Lily, Lady Wflhism Bereeford, now a widow for the third time, was originally MiSEIPriCP. who became first Mrs. Hammere- , ler, and then Duchess of Marlborough. I Trousseaux, as regards quantity, grow small by deorees and beautifully less, many a society bride ' deems half a dozen gowns—including her wedding dress —snfficient,with cloaks and headgear,in like proportion. The money that at one time would have been expended upon an elaberate outfit is now placed to the bride's account, and this she uses later when and how she pleases, with the advantage of being thoroughly up-to- date an impossibility where an extensive trousseau is indulged in. The lengthy honeymoon is also ancient history, a run to Paris for a week or two being much preferred to the old-fashioned idea of a month or so's solitude in a country house. Fine black cloth skirts are much worn just now, these I blending admirably with the pretty crêpe de chine blouses, which the smartest among us are patronising so extensively. Black crêpe de chine is one of those fabrics which possesses the very hippy knack of making the wearer appear more slim than she really is, moreover it suggests mourning without any overwhelming gloom. Considering its light texture it does no: wear badly, and takes kindly to almost any form of trimming. Some wemen subetitue fricze for cloth, be skirts to wear with teeee dainty blouses, but the idea is not a happy one, as regards a harmonious whole. White violets promise to be very favorite blooms with leading milliners this coming summer, both the Neapoli- tan, and wood variety. Prim little bunches,surroanded with leaves of Wank silk are in preparation, for placing under the brims of hats and tucking int 3 rufflm, and I these nromise to be much more fascinating wear than 'the li4e hunclits of purple violets with which we are all rather too familiar. White roses too will shortly be substitute d for those of sable hue, great earnitures of these fiewers appear on evening gowns destined to be worn in a few weeks time, and the mounts for hats, built of monster specimens, are particularly effective. 1 A. common error in hanging pictures is that of placing them too high. As a matter of fact the centre of the picture should be on a line with the eyes of a person of average height, and if the picture is very long Pnd narrow, it should be slightly lower. Then, pictures ehot.lld be grouped, and not arranged in long plain rows, or dotted evenly on each wall expanse in the room. They should be placed in a pretty uneven group. Whore the pictures are of the same kind and framing identical, then a rest- ful undecorated piece of wall should be left before placing another group. To remove stains from silver, the pulp cf a lemon, whose juice has been used for lemon-squash, is bath ii fficient and harmless. Indian silver and brass is always I cleaned by the natives with lemons or limes.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
