      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>711 E FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>711 E FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>nEv. J. a. GREINHOUGH, ILL</p>
        <p>- The well-known Baptist .divine of Leicester 'enters on his duties as President of the Nations Free Church Council at Cardiff on March 12. His itniugural address is being anticipated with keen interest. There is no more effective plat- form orator in the country than Mr. Greenhough. lie preaches and lectures without manuscript •or scrap of notes, but not, it need- not be said, Without very careful preparation. Mr. Green- bough was a great success as President of the Baptist Union in 1895. His volume of sermons, "The Cross in Modern Life," went into two editions. Mr. Greenhough sometimes looks absent-minded, but he is all there., One of his personal characteristics is his carelessness as -to dress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CARDIFF MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>THE CARDIFF MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Cardiff has grown into a town of consider- -able importance within a comparatively few "years, and now ranks as the capital of Wales. Nonconformity is predominant in the town, Which possesses a vigorous Free Church Council and many eloquent preachers. Celts of all sorts are noted for their gracious hospi- _</p>
        <p>tality, and the kindly Welshmen are making the most careful arrangements for the comfort -of the thousand and more guests—representa- tives to the National Free Church Council— who will shortly visit them. The Cardiff meetings, coming at:the close of the great Simul- ta:neous Mission, are bound to be of special Interest. Not the least popular of the sessions will be that at which the Rev. John McNeill, Dr. Forsyth, and Gipsy Smith will narrate their mission experiences. GIPSY SMITH.</p>
        <p>The British Weekly has published a most interesting sketch of Gipsy Smith, who is un- Aloubtedly at the present moment the most Popular evangelist in the world. Wherever he Roes he crowds the largest building in the Place. Gipsy Smith's father is still living, a very hale, active old man of well over seventy, with not a grey hair in his head. He is employed as an evangelist in the Eastern counties and elsewhere. Gipsy Smith is never weary of speak- ing of his father's beautiful character. " To zny father's example," he says, "I owe every- thing." Since 1897 Mr. Smith has worked as a rnissioner in connection with the National Free Church Council. During the mission, which has just. closed, his meetings have been very large and most successful. ROYALTY AND THE FREE OHDRCHES.</p>
        <p>• The Free .Churches greatly esteemed and Toyed Queen ,Victoria., Her Majesty was •tolerant in her religious views. . She expected :all her household, down to her humblest kitchen servants, to attend some place of worship, but they might go to whatever church or chapel they pleased. And Many of them -vere NonComformists. The late Queen had no 'sympathy with the bigotry and intolerance .of the highest High Anglicanism. "I hope, Mr. she said to a Ritualistic clergy- man, "that you will get on well with the Non- Fonformists. You know you will have to get on with them in Heaven." It is well-known 'that the Queen, when in Scotland, ,regularly .1% ttended a Presbyterian Church, and her relations with Saadi' rtbsbytergii seem to have been more intimate</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>_ With Anglicans.</head>
        <p>_ With Anglicans.</p>
        <p>KING EDWARD'S ATTITUDE. , The new King is not less tolerant than was .1118 revered and lamented mother. It is inte- resting to recall that on the occasion of the Bialnond Jubilee service the Prince of Wales, 418 he then was, insisted that some places on the stops of St. Paul's Cathedral should be iStrietly reserved for representatives of the .-Free Churches. It is probable that the • .4org.t Idsation called the Three Denominations will vlsim its ancient right of approach to the "Throne for the presentation of an address. IhTs body vr as founded in 1727, and consists of ,11:tiltiSt, Congregational, and Preshytorit7l iei..isturs residing in and about the cities of .Len(lot and Westminster. The Methodists 'were not in existence when the organisAtion founded, and have not been inclu•lod. He' ever, the President ef the Wesleyan • lereuce is recoived.at Court.</p>
        <p>411t:LIOANS AND THE SIMULTANEOUS IMIT•ISOs A partimlarly pleasant feature of the inissioit liltst ended was the hearty co-operation giveit a few Anglican clergymen. The tho • 'ran of the Evangelical party in tho 'Church of England, wrote: "It was a gre,t unrtaking, conducted, we believe, in no 'nectsrian spirit; but rather with the view oi •aws!oning the careless." Perhaps more signi- tic.nit still was the attitude of the St. James's Gaze/ te, which is Church and Conservative to the core. Said the St. -James's : "The spiritual-</p>
        <p>rnin(lod among the adherents of the National Church will not regard this siniultanenns inission 'with suspicion, but will rather welcome .it as an evidence of sincerity and zeal no loss strenuous than their own." "MACLAREN OF MANCHESTER." Dr. Alexander Maclaren, the famous Man- -rhester divine, has celebrated his seven ty-ti it.h lirihday, and has received a message of congra- tulation from the Baptist ministers of Vianches- :ter and District. During the last year 13r. Mac- lar.r, has been Unusually active, and his hea!t'i, °T•• the whole, has been exceedingly 'TII(4 public platform has had more of his Drk.once, while from his .own pulpit' he lir,s Drenched with unusual regularity. A GREAT LOSS To ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>'rho Bev. a Campbell Morgan will cross tho Atl;trtjc soon after the simultaneous miin .: la over to: take up part of the late D. L. , Mood's work at Northfield. Mr. Morgan's , great services during the mission have made the Free Churches realise thereby how sorioni IS the loss they ava sustaining and how valuable a" acquisition America is gaining. Mr. Morgan's Bible readings, which are really ex- Periences of the deepest truths of Scripture, were attended by audiences of a thousand and rnore persons. And this at nine in the morn- lng ! How many other ministers in the country Would accomplish such an achievement? Mr. Morgan's manner in the pulpit is generally very calm and quiet, but at times it is intensely dramatic, and it is always effective. In private Mr. Morgan is simple, unaffected and kind. l'ree Churchmen in England console themselves for his loss by hoping and believing that he taay make frequent visits to our shores.</p>
        <p>.DRAUG'H'</p>
