      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>IN THE GRAY DAWN OF THE CENTURY.</head>
        <p>IN THE GRAY DAWN OF THE CENTURY.</p>
        <p>The passing of the 19th century. bringing to us the dawn of a century that we all feel will be full of possibilities such as the mind of men has not yet dreamt of, is causing all sorts and conditions of men to guess and ponder as to what these possi- bilities may be. Areerican papers at this time seem to vie with each other in trying to get hold of the views of prominent citizens on questions of national and international iniportance, and chief ' among these is the question, of Great Britain's standing among the industrial nations of the world: To one built after, the manner of the present ' writer the matter of Britain's' ability, or otherwise to keep the lead in industrial affairs or in Intel- lectual pursuits always appeals with great force, and reteeences, to tt e subject gain atteu'i W bile realising, or accepting it as One of the facts of life that in socialistic thought lies the germ possibiloy or potential power, call it what we may, for ridding us of he almost devilish idea that the competitive systeei in industry, and the building of warships and the maintenance of stand- ing armies are necessary to our comfort and well- being. I still feel that it is better to go along slowly, do things surely, and so reach the goal the humani- talian holds up as an ideal before us. Yes,we must accept and deal with things as they are, so long as we have such a vast mass of humanity among us given over entirely to the pursuit of subjects that are of no earthly use,and bring with them no high, or elevating influence with which to work out the redemption of man. Going back no farther than the old Chartist days the workingman has been bewailing and lamenting his:sad plight of ignorence. Ms constant demand has been for more leisure, for more opportunities for self culture. He has felt, and known that in his ignorance lay the secret of his weakness, and yet with all this knowledge of his own short com- ings, are we not justified in asking what has be done to rid himself of the horrid capitalistic class, or' caste that grinds and moulds him to suit its own evil purpose? lam not now referring to any nation, or any particular class of workmen. So far as my experience goes, and it is a pretty wide one, what I have said applies to the whole of the labour world. I would even include, and put in the front row,the men with the ability to make money and to control their fellowmen, but their ignorance of the ethics and sesthetics of life is so overshadewed by this ability that as a rule they are lost sight of, and are'not counted in our profits and loss account.ln the United States you will hear one long conceited pean of self-congratulation on the lines of having all the perfections, and none, or few, of the imperfections I attendant on ignorance, and yet, what is the true state of the case ? Judged by the way that strikes and labour disputes generally are carried out, the sort of stuff you have in the municipal government ' of such cities as New York, Chicago, and San Francisco, no nation on earth displays sudli a be- wildering amount of dense and stupid ignorance. It is only fair to add that, from my own stand- point, not believing in manhood, or universal suf- frage, this may be largely attributed to the intro- duction to the ballot box of material acquainted with nothing much better than the ideas of the middle ages. I would like to define the ignorance to which I am devoting so much self conceited attention. If I point out to a friend all the benefits that would accrue to him and his fellows by the universal acceptance of co-operation and its potentialities, and he neither defends himself by argument, nor yet accepts my propositions, i at once,and perhaps rather rudely, tell him he is an ignorant ass. Or again, if I point out to the workingman the folly he commits by his intemperance: how he is simply building up a rich class at the price and cost of the labour ;'that what he helps to do, each time he gets a little drop too much, is simply to maintain crowds of jails, crowds of judges, magistrates, and policemen, and he still keeps on indulging in his little weakness, why, I simply call him a besotted fool, and go away moaning over such ignorance. The ignorant man may be found in the palace as well as the hut. What we are more concerned with is he whom Markham had in mind, when he wrote his poem " The man with the hoe," I commenced this article with a reference to the extreme anxiety displayed by American writers, and the European, or should I say British corre- spondents of American papers as to the future of British industries. In noting this ex- treme solicitude about Britain's welfare, I question myself as to its why, and wherefore, how in seek- ing for this reason there is no need to jump to the conclusion that all Britain's critics are her enemies. That would be as big a mistake as to fancy that, only among her critics may she find her friends Not in the West will Britain find the friend whom Timon of Athens sought. No, the Westerner is absorbed with only two ideas, he would have Cali- fornia the greatest State in the Union, and San Francisco the greatest city in the greatest State. Nature's work in the State is all right, it is man's work that.is all wrong. Another case of ignorance I know not if lam right, but our critics seem to have a great desire to see all the big work of the world come to America, and a big share of it come to San Francisco. They argue that "England is a nearly extinct industrial volcano, beeause she has almost exhausted her once great mines of coal and iron. As soon as they are no longer capable of being profitably worked, British industrial supremacy must disappear as surely as the lamp must go, out when the oil is consumed. The wick and. all the mechanism may be perfect, but without oil a lamp cannot be made to burn." I have before me quite a mass of figures relating to the iron and coal question in Great Britain. but at this time I do not care to deal with them. What is of quite as much interest just now, is the refer- ence in the above quotation to the wick and mechan- ism of the lamp being perfect. It is a question I enter upon with a little misgiving, as I have more than once, received from my English friends kindly warnings to say as little as possible touching the English workman, and his methods of work, but, as we say, desperate diseases require desperate remedies. Twenty years ago I realized, the un- palatable truth that workingmen must individually and through their organizations leave the question of methods of work to other and more scientific heads, contenting themselves only with the, to them, central idea as to their remuneration. The answer I invariably received was, " that extra labour must have extra pay." The answer to my assertion was all right. The fact was we failed to approach the question in the calm spirit needed, hence the collision. Now what are the facts; from Lord Rosebery down, the confession is made that the antiquated methods of industrial training had almost resulted in commercial disaster, and all points in the industrial world must be reconsidered. His Lordship was emphatic, and to the peint when he told the British people, that "they must be pre- pared to fight for their very existence." •- - The Lord Mayor of London in a recent address welcomed American capital. He thought it a good thing that the Yankee ehould lay out his capital in fixing up the Britisher in modern electric notions. He may now further congratulate his fellow citi- zens on the certainty that Yankee capital will now , put Yankee ferry boats on the Thames. If he is also able to add to his felicitations the one, that all he profits from these ventures will be spent among he English people why all will be well. But why hould'ut English capital and English labour be squal to the task of supplying English wants? We ball find the American answer to this question hortly. Something in the way ef satisfaction to he Britisher may be the knowledge that British apital runs American breweries, .Englishmen should realise what the American continent means,</p>
