      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>to. Before tbe first decade of the century will have passed America will have one hundred Million people.' It now requires small imaginative powers to realize what her productiveness will be. On the Ame-ican railroads in 1899 there were used 33,595 passenger coaches, and the immensity of her factories, also, what the demands or wants of her 80 millions people amount and 1,24426 freight cases. The production of pig, iron in America for 1898 was nearly equal to that of its two greatest competitors, and the most of the output was conQumed within its own borders. Not to prolong this lino of thought, we have, the state- ment of one of America's ablest eons Chauncy Depew that the American people now , produce .£2,000,000.000 worth of goods more than they can consume. When I am told that American manu- facturers are only getting what English manufac- turers cannot supply because of being already so full of orders, I refuse to believe it. I noted with some surrrise a Christmas appeal being made in the " Examiner " in aid of the iron—workers of War- rington. Was • this in consequence of a glut of orders,or,as a result of a firm of Clyde ship-builders having placed orders for 150,000 tons of plates in the United--States at a saving of .£50,000? Are the scotch iron-masters damping their furnaces, and talking of closing their steel works indefinitely because of having too many orders on their books ? I guess not. The "London Express" says the British " Artizans vie in obstinate conservatism with their employers, and apply to their vocation the same spirit as led their forefathers to roust and break up machinery sixty years ago." From my own per- sonal experience I can endorse this statement. Yet I cannot for a moment think that dearness of Bride) labour,or its inaptness are to blame,the real truth is,-and the Britii-h people must acknowledge it,/ toe Anglo-Saxon is not confined to the British Isles, nor is his action any more limited by British</p>
        <p>lug of winning foreign markets it says :—`• It is by the development of the most efficient men in the workshops and all along the line to the ultimate maker of great contracts. The character of the British workman, who was for a long period the most efficient in the world—as Carlyle expressed it " the strongest, the cunningest, and the willingest our world ever had "—is suffering gravely from the restrictions imposed on him by the trades unions. These limit the amount of work he shall do, restrict him in the use of machinery, and lessen his chance of individual development. For this reason among others, the distinction of the greatest efficiency measured in the worth of his product has passed to the American workman." I oelieve bhis to be only a partial,trutb. As I have said above the Anglo- Saxon is fetpad holding the directing hand in eight out of every ten of American workshops. I might even say the controlling spirit in American work- shops is British born. I could duplicate the charge made against the English trade unionist with a hundred quotations I have at, my hand, but what's the use? I have nothing to say in favour of the so-called "development of the most efficient men in the workshops." I see proofs every day that the mere development of that side of man means any- thing but the expanding of the Ego-Altruistic side of man. In concluding this already too long article I give another quotation from a writer who realizes what this race for wealth means, but what can be done while men are learning all that irresponsible selfish competition mans. "The American habit is to make sport of the - British workman because he is easy in his move- ments. As if in America we were all enamoured with hard work,all supremely happy in rushing. We know that we are not enchanted wlth toil and high speed, but, forsooth,we make a pretence of enchant- ment and have fashioned a ' Shibboleth ' about keeping up with the procession, Now, the British Workman says that the procession must go at his pace not he at the procession's. Herein is he a sen- sible man. He knows what the American pretence is worth and be will have none of it. Instead of sneering at the British workman for taking many holidays and for insisting on a half holiday every Saturday and for having curious ideas about a time for breakfast and a time for tea, the American workman could do better to turn the laugh on himself, unless he is really contented with his lot, in which case he should cease from striking and let trades unions go to the doge. Some alleged political economists in the United i States pretend to think t a very glorious thing because American workmen work more hours per day and turn out more product than the Englisher, as if the end of all things, the ultimate aim of American man, were to work longer, and to do more work than any other Christian or any Pagan! What is the real state of the case? Simply this that the American workman is compelled to work harder and to work longer than his British cousin. If there is any merit in this, whose is it. Perhaps the alleged economist will answer." HENRY WHITHAM. San Francisco.</p>
        <p>Conservatism. Two questions now confront us,and I may preface them by repeating some words used many years ago by the Warrington wire manufacturers: "Trade once diverted seldom returns to its original chan- nels." Thwe who have studied trade questions know thi- to be true. The two questions we have before us have reference to trade organisations and dearness of labour. Never was trade unionism so strong, and so determined as it is to-day in the US. An outsider like myself may be forgiven if he looks at it with some misgivings. A feeling comes over me at times as of something dreadful going to happen. Everybody and everything seems to be organising. To such lengths has this gone that the Industrial Committee sitting at Washington gathered from its witnesses that labour unions con- trolled state legislation by means of a black list,and tbat the legislators are afraid of the labour vote,and they do not hesitate to say so. From the skilled artizan away up to the unskilled cobbler all are de- termined to have a "fair day's wage for a fair day's work." The cobbler says be will have a dollar for every pair of shoes he soles, and the teamster de- , mends and gets 14s a day of twelve hours, and 2s for each hour of overtime after that. I have said tbat all labour is organised, this is not quite so. Whilertbe hod -carrier demands and gets 12s a day of eigbt hours through his union, and the city cor- poration has decided to give the street sweeper 8s a day of eight hours, there are yet others to whom organisation seems impossible and to whom are denied the protection of the city authorities. I refer to the large mass of humanity to whom I have the honour to belocg. and who are known as common labourers. Here I must give an incident in my own happenings, as it has a bearing on the strike, or lock-out, whichever it may be, reported in the "Examiner,' and which I have read carefully through. The Crosfield Com- pany acknowledge the right of their workmen to organize, but refuse to have dealings with a repre- sentative. This is but consistent. It is according to the teachings of what is known as the Manches- ter School, free trade all round. The right of the strong to crush the weak, and so on, all for the magnificent sum of less tbal4 7 dollars Lir 72 hours' work, and less than 9 dollars for 96 hours a week. e gods, hear it, added to this surprising liberality is a week's holiday accompanied with a weeks wage. I should say if the men take up with the terms offered they would make first-class absent- minded beggars, and that—" while he saved the Empire his employer (would) save his place." Now to my own little episodes, I work for one of the largest corporations in the States. A consider- able portion of the labour is, if not skilled, at least responsible. Of course,a deal of the labour is skilled 80 far it is all protected by powerful Brotherhoods, or, in plain talk, Trade Unions. Below this comes the unskilled or common labour,gaining its recruits from:the freshly arrived emigrant; those who cannot do, or de not like too hard work, and the generally unsuccessful,such as your bumble servant for instance. ihe great rise in wages has, of course been accompanied by a comparatively much greater increase in the price of eatables, and, indeed all that one buys into the home. NI common fellows, who had no union were feeling it pretty hard. We could make no demand, we had no union to back even a simple request for more wages. What should we do ? Secret whisperings did no good. There, seemed no one in the whole crowd who would take the lead. I watched the whole business as it moved along .step by step, I had lost one situation since I came into the city through forming a Trade Union, I determined not to run the risk agair. At length the questions came to me, couldn't something be done ? and they came pretty thick. I didn't know I couldn't saw what should be done. I was feeling the hard times. Plenty of work at Ss a day didn't suit me any more than it did the other mete and I thought a great many thinks. At length When no one else seemed disposed to start the thing. I deter- mined that if I had to bell the cat I would do it in my own way. I wrote out a petition to the Company, and then told the foreman what I was after, and asked his help. He promised to introduce a deputation to the Superintendetit if I could get one together It took me two days to get two men with sufficient • courage to face the terrible super. At length we got the petition into his hands. After reading it, and kindly giving us a most gracious smile, he promised to forward it to "the powers that be," and if he had an answer he would let us know. Two weeks went by and no answer, three weeks and no answer, and then I got plenty of chaff, some of it rather grim. I hadn't gene the right road about it and so on. But the day came when I received an answer. All that we, or I, asked for had been granted, and / was autho- rised to tell the men. I at once called a meeting and told the men how I had gone about.and how it had resulted. I was, for a day, the most popular man in San Francisco, and the men made me a Christmas gift of £25. Now here was a case where a large body of men, for want of organised method, Could come to no definite conclusion, and where a spokesman from their own body ran a good chance of being made a victim. Men who have to ask the boss for permission to join the union will make poor unionists, and an organised body that cannot insist upon its properly appointed officials carrying out its decisions might as well not exist. There is another side to this. The democratic lines upon which a union is run very often lead to trouble. The rank and file abound with men who never give a moment to the study of labour ques- tions, and, I am afraid to add that they very often appoint as their officials men whose onfy ability is their fluency of speech. Representatives of labour must have an international knowledge of all ques- tions relating to labour, and of the mobile nature of capital. A British labour representative should know something of the reasons why a struggle, should be now on between America and Austrian capitalists for the order to construct a large electric plant in London. Max O'Rell says "The ignorance of nations on the subject of what is going on in other nations is something appalling" Another Max says of Britian "Perhaps her greatest need is the knowledge that her commercial supremacy is leaving her." Of the truth ot the first of these quotations I have not the least doubt. Indeed it would be hard to doubt it after one has made a comparism of the output in any given line of pro- duction in the two countries. The truth of the latter quotation remains to be probed. Mr. Pete Curran a delegate of the British Trades Unions to the American Federation of Labour ad- dressing a meeting in New York said : "Your employers are getting more work out of the workingmen than are the employers in the old country, and the manner in which you rush in this country would not be tolerated for a single moment in England. There is one thing I want to impress upon your minds, and that is that your manufac- turers are dumping their commodities in our market cheaper than they can be made over there. That goes to show that you are more efficient slaves', What Mr. Curran says of the American workman is a truth, but it is one of those truths that has to be reckoned with. The American workman is an "efficient slave." I take in an American monthly—" The World's Work." If it had a good circulation in England among the workers, it would do some good. Speak-</p>
        <p>IHE ENAM 'NEE, FRI Y, FEBRUARY 22, 1901</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT A WIRE ROPERY. Compensation 'Awarded.</head>
        <p>THE ACCIDENT AT A WIRE ROPERY. Compensation 'Awarded.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday,last week his Honour (Judge W. Bowen Rowlands, K.C.) delivered judgment in the matter of the claim for compensation, brought by Charles Thomas Rigby, of 14, David-street, Warrington,assistant overlocker at the Warrington Wire Rope Works against his employers. Mr. Browne again appeared for the applicant and Mr. Leslie Scott, barrister (instructed by Messrs Field, Son and Broomfields,of Liverpool) for the respondents. It will be remembered that it was a claim under the Workmen's Compensation Act, 1897, for £lB 12s fld and 17e 6d per week for life for injuries sustained while following his usual employment. The claim was made up as follows :-174 61 per week from the 9th July, 1900, to January 17th, 1901 (27 weeks), £lB 12s 61, and 178 61 for life. The facts, as stated by applicant' solicitor at the hearing, were as follows i—Applicang was an assistant overlooker in the employ of the respont dents at the time of the accident. He was injured on- the 25th June last, whilst replacing the belt on a pulley on the 'abaft in the fine wire room in the respondents' works, his clothes being caught in an adjoining pulley, and he himself taken round the shaft. His left knee was so badly injured that amputation below the knee was necessary; the knee and ankle of his right leg and the thumb of his right hand were also injured. In addition, the tight elbow and the ribs were injured. The average weekly earnings of the applicant during the twelve months previous to the injury had been 353. The respondents claimed that the applicant was an ordinary workman and not an assistant ever- looker, that the average weekly earnings were 15s 2d per week and no more, and the respondents did not ad- mit that the applicant was or since the accident had been able to earn practically "nil." His Honour on Thursday morning briefly summed up the evidence, and remarked that the whole of the wages of the applicant were eerned in or about a factory with. in the meaning of the Ant. All the work in the various sheds was partly one and the same business and carried on in the same premises, which were surrounded by a high common wall and approached by one entrance only Employment was under the same employers, and it was continuous for 18 months. His wages amounted on an average to something like 303 21 a week. He (the judge) awarded the applicant 15s 11 a week with costs on C scale. •</p>
      </div>
