      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SPEAKE -ASP</head>
        <p>THE SPEAKE -ASP</p>
        <p>Dr, Williams' Pink Pills for are not a Purgative sie</p>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY CHAMPIONSHIPS. _— Splendid Running over a Splendid Course. Local Successes. That the interest and excitement connected with this great cross-country event is once more gaining its old popularity, few will gainsay, seeing that the attendance at Haydock Park on Saturday was as near 5,000 as 4,000. Strangely uneventful has been the Northern for some years, and we shall have to look back ten years or so to find a Northern championship characterised by such stirring doings as the one on Saturday. The weather was gloriously fine,the venue a slendid one, and the Association are indebted to the Racecourse Company for providing such cosy accommodation for the runners. Although both races were by no means considered " open," Dove being the popular fancy for the Junior, and J T Rimmer and W Ashton the more favoured for the Senior, the issue created quite an old-time " furore," and was a fruitful topic for wordy warfare. Our van-, able climate made the going nice and sof t,with the :mit that Rimmer became all the rage for the Senior whilst Dove, who had been tried to be half-a-mile better than last year's winner (R E Hughes), was barred in certain quarters for the Junior, in which twenty-one clubs took part, the number of runners being 185. They presented a stirrieg spectacle passing the stand for the first time. Approaching the winning post after the first lap thei Gateshead team were going great guns, having five men in the first dozen, the leaders, being: T Devlin (Sankey), R Hughes (Gateshead), F G Harris (Leeds P.C.), J W Thompson (Gateshead), G Southward (Sutton), T Dove (Gateshead), C Beresford (Oldham), and R Dillon (Gateshead). The time for the first lap was 10min. 455ees. There was quite a transformation scene in the second circuit. T Dove of Gateshead,' a popular fancy, and favourite for last year's junior at Rotherham, was out on his own with his fellow clubmen going well. De was followed av by F G Harris (Leeds P.C.) T Devlin (Sankey), R E Hughes,and J W Thompson (Gateshead). The field was spread-eagled at half distance, at which point the race looked a certainty for Dove, who did not run last year owing to an accident, whilst bis fellow members were the most prominent of the others. Entering the last lap Dove was three hundred yards in front of Harris (Leeds), with Devlin close behind, and Hughes and Thompson (Gateshead) running level,and T Clark (Skerton) sixth. It was a long tail-end, Dove being nearly a mile in front of the last batch, the time being 30min. 34seo. The further Dove went the better he seemed to enjoy it, and he finished as frah as a daisy quite 400 yards in front of the second man, Devlin of (Sankey), with F Harris (Leeds) a good third. RESULTS. The Junior Championship. FIRST TWENTY. M. S. Pos'n</p>
        <p>T Dove, Gateshead T Devlin, Sankey It G Harris, Leeds PC 4l 49 3 .1 w Thotnpson, Gateshead R E Hughes, Gateshead J Gardner, Gateshead 42 14— 6 ' T Clark, Skerton C Beresford, Oldham 9 Marian&amp; Oldham 42 23 9 E Hilton, Sankey J W Holland, Leeds G Eastwood, Sankey 42 31-12 J R Howarth, Hallamshire E Lucas, Gateshead 42 39-14 R Dillon, Gateshead F Webber, Sutton 42 42-18 C E Kisby, Sheffield 42 52-17 H Hodson, Broughton 42 53-18 J Daintaith, Bolton P A Burrow, Leeds P C 43 10-20 THE CLUB PLACING. Gateshead Congregational.—T Dove 1, J W Thomp- son 4, R E Hughes 5, J Gardner 6, E Lucas 14, R Dillon 15 ' • total, 45-1. Sankey H.—T Devlin 2, E Hilton 10, G Eastwood 12, J Wilding 25. Riley, 37, Prince, 55 ; total, 141 points-2. Oldham.—C Beresford 8, Marlow 9, Platt 35, Gill 36, Atkins 45, 11 Beresford 48 ; total, 181-3. , Bolton Primrose, 261, 4 ; Broughton H and AC. 265</p>
        <p>5 ; Leeds P C, 296, 6 ; Hallamshire H and A C, 310, 7 ; Sutton, 319, 8 ; Skerton. 395, 9 ; Rochdale, 433, 10 ; St. Helens Sacred Heart's, 438, 11 ; Stalvbridge, 442, 12 ; Sheffield United, 448, 13 ; Runcnrn, 472, 14 ; Winning. ton Park, 506, 15 ; Eccles H, 575, 16. Liverpool Pem- broke, North Manchester, Dalton Cycling Club, and Macclesfield did not finish. Distance 7 miles.</p>
        <p>The Senior Championship. The Seniors were dispatched at 4.50. Ashton (Sal- ford) breaking the lino and setting a good pace, accom panied by J Mather (Warrington), and Pugh, Morris,and Mundy (Salford): The leaders were soon a long way in front, and passing the stand the first time the order was Bennison (Manchester), W Ashton (Sal(ord), and H Baker (Salford), who were almost level, .1 Roberts (Sef- ton), J T Rimmer (Southport),A F Wilson (Airedale),R B Pugh (Salford), A Preston (Dalton), T P Peach (Crewe), in the order their names are written, forming the next squad ; time, 10min 353ec. Rimmer came clear away in the last lap and finished quite fresh 60 yards in front of Ashton, with Morris (Crewe) third. Tennant was fourth, and Hossack fifth. Rimmer's time was 61mIn lsec. It was an easy win for the favourite, who found the going much more to his liking than the slush at Rotherham. The officials did their work in a masterly manner, and the crowd had a splendid view from the stands. Haydock Park is an ideal venue for a cross- country contest, although those who prefer heavy going and ploughed fields may share a different opinion.</p>
        <p>FIRST FORTY. M. S. Pos'n J T Rimmer, Southport . 6l 7 1 W Ashcan, Salford H J Morris, Crewe B 6l 42 3 .1 Tennant,, West Cheshire H</p>
        <p>T Hossack, Crowe H 6l 56 5 R Cheaters, Crewe H 62 2 6 J Roberts, Sefton H 62 3 7 J Pickup, Salford H A Preston, Dalton A A A 62 37 9 H Lawson Leads A C R C Pugh, Salford H A E Barker, Leeds A C 62 57-12 T P Peach, Crewe H 63 3-13 G Coles, Rotherham H 63 5-14 J Brady. Salford H H Barker, Salford El 63 29-16 M Greene, Se! too H A Stubbs. Crewe H R W Sherratt, Liverpool H and A C 63 42-19 H Lewis, Crewe H 63 44-20 C Souch, Salford El B Bennison, Manchester H 64 7-22 J W Lees, Salford H 64 19-23 J W Robinson, Leeds A C J A M'Loughlin, Salford H 64 49-25 C.N Snowden, Manchester H 64 54-26 H Harrison, Manchester H 64 56-27 W Jones, Farnwortb H C Large, Salford H P Brady, Farnworth E H Morrie, Salford II 65 5-31 F Townley, Crewe Tally-Ho W Williams, Sef ton H J Pearson, Bolton H 65 19-34 J Griffiths, Sefton 13 65 24-35 J Mather, Warrington H 65 42-36 11 Elobson, Rotherham H 65 43-37 Id Preston, Dalton I A A 65 47-38 A Kershaw, Leeds AC El 66 1-39 W Barker, Leeds A C THE CLUB PLACINGS.</p>
        <p>Crewe—J Morris 3, T Hossack 5, R Cheaters 6, T P Peach 13, A Stubbs 18, H Lewis 20; total 65-1. Salford—W Ashton 2, J Pickup 8. R B Pugh 11, J Brady 15, H Barker 16, C &amp;Rich 21; total 73-2 Leeds A C—H Lawson 10. A E Barker 12, J W Rob,n• son 24, A Kershaw 39, W Barker 40, 11 Ruddock 1; total 166-3 fiefton—J Roberts 7, 51 Greene 17, W Williams 33, J Griffiths 35, W Longaiore 54, J Stoddart 56; total 202-4. Manchester Harriers—B Bennison 22. C N Snowden (captain) 26, H Harrison 27, W H Whitiogalow 46, J Cooper 51, J Lawton 68: total 240-5. Dalton—A Preston 9, M Pr3ston 39, S Hughes 43, T Longmore bO;T Corntlawaite 53, W Parkinson 65; total 258-6.</p>
        <p>Warrington, 322 points, 7 • Bolton, 335, 8 ; Rotherham, 342, 9; Crewe Tally-Ho, 376, 10; West Cheshire, 450, 11; Liverpool H and AC, 525, 12. Airedale, Farnworth, Cheshire Tally-Ho' Liverpool Gymnasium and South- port did not finish a team, whilst none of the Airedale team completed the course. Distance 10 miles.</p>
