        <p>CoueAGE in a Chan- NewTaxati Sources of cellor of the Exchequer, on. when matched with discre- tion, will lead him far on the right path, but it almost needs audacity at the present moment to propose new taxes. Theoretically, it is believed that :we have attained a nice balance between direct and indirect taxation, between imposts which reach the upper, the middle, and the wage- earning classes. Any new taxes would, it is thought, disturb this balance,besides creating fresh opposition, and they could not as readily be taken off again as an increase upon existing taxes. On the other hand, it has to be maintained that, with an increase in expenditure, growing year by year apart from temporary causes, some new sources of revenue are imperative. They will have to be found, and now is the time to find them. The conditions required are that the new tax shall not touch any necessary of life, but shall deal with something largely in use, easily assessable and upon which the profits are large. What objects are there which answer to these conditions? Not one of the older objects of taxation, relieved in the I time Of Mr. GLADSTONB, would seem to be suit- able. Mineral waters appear to be the best objects we know of for a new experiment. They were never used in such quantities and varieties as now. They are not a necessary of life. They do not come into almost every household, as do tea, coffee, tobacco, beer, wines, and spirits. They employ a large amount of capital and labour, and they are rather an article of luxury than of necessity. The varieties of mineral waters may be divided into two kinds—home and foreign. There is no licence to manufacture and sell. There is no special task that touches them. They are sold by a large variety of tradesmen. Many varieties come from abroad and enter into competition with home products, some purely dietetic in value and others distinctly medicinal in character. Why should they not be taxed? A very small tax would bring in a considerable revenue, and it should be easy enough to collect. We believe that a tax of a halfpenny a pint all round would neither injure the trade nor have an injurious effect upon consumption. A discrimin- ating tax against foreign products is not advisable but it might be desirable to increase:the duty to a penny a pint on distinctly medicinal waters com- ing from abroad. If any better tax can be found we should like to hear of it. Taxes on cycles, autocars, and theatre tickets have been suggested many times only to be rejected. A tax on matches, as we all remember, was proposed with disastrous results. We class matches with necessaries, and no Chancellor" of the Exchequer would be audacious enough to repeat a calamitous experiment. Surveying the entire field, we can discover nothing so suitable as mineral waters. It is hardly to the point that these are non-alcoholic, and some :of them are freely consumed by abstainers from alcohol. The bulk of ordinary mineral waters pass into con- sumption along with wines and spirits, and the revenue from them, if a small tax were imposed, would come from the middle and upper classes,and but slightly from the consumers of lemonade and ginger-beer. It is probable the yield of a tax upon mineral waters would be from one enillion to two millions, and, of course, it would fall upon the con- sumer and not on the manufacturer. If consump- tion were slightly checked, we do not know that there would be any loss in consequence. Some mineral waters are hardly what they profess to be and it is ridiculous to urge that an injury would follow a slight tax, either to the manufacturer or his workpeople, or the general public. All that is required is a little courage, and the community would be benefited in the end. When nearly all drinks are taxed, the exemption of mineral waters cannot be logically or permanently maintained.</p>
        <p>E Scorn for the House of TH House of Corn- Commons. mons does not main- tain the position it has a right to hold in the working of the Government for the time being and in the general Constitution. Ministers regard it as an inconvenient body, and they have essayed to reduce its privileges and its authority in consequence. We have had a Minis- ter for War, the head of a great spending depart- ment, in the House of Lords, much to the incon- venience of the representatives of the people. We have now the head of the navy in the Lords, and the Postmaster-General and the Secretary for Foreign Affairs. Under-Secretaries have to answer fer great departments of the State. The navy is not badly represented, bat the Foreign Office is only represented by the Premier's son, who is an Under-Secretary, and who cannot ans- wer supplementary qUestions, in consequence of a rule, not made by the House of Commons, but by the Leader of the House. The Post Office has no direct representative, and the Financial Secretary to the Treasury, as in a previous Parliament, has simply to read out the official answers of the per- manent staff. Lord SALISBURY in thus indicating how little he cares for the Commons is true to his general characteristics. He is a kind of Grand Lama. When he was:, Foreign Secretary he managed our foreign affairs in his own way, and he does not seem to have even consulted his Cabinet except when affairs were critical. The private member of the House of Commons has been made war against ever since the Unionist party came into power. He is an inconvenience who has to be suppressed as much as possible. His time is taken away from him on the plea of</p>
        <p>necessity, and the sessions have been shortened at the same time, so that the Government is all in all. Only in the way of moving amendments to - the Address and in asking questions can he exer- cise the right of free criticism, for which he is sent to Westminster. The usual motions by which ho could convey his ideas and.ventilate questions not yet ripe for legislation are now few and far between. When an occasion does arise the Government Whips and followers do their best to bring about a count-out, and then refer to the event as proof of the little interest private mem- bers take in their old Parliamentary privileges. In the ordinary debates the Ministry discourages independent opinion. Members are warned not to speak too freely. It often happens that only one speech will be saade in reply, and that will come from a Minister. The whole art of Parliamentary rule has now, under Mr. BALFOUR'S leadership and LORD SALISBURY'S Premierehip, come to mean the reduction of politicians to items and voters. Ministers, in effect, say to their supporters in the Commons "We don't want your opinions but your votes. We prefer you to be silent, to bring in no measures of your own, and to be our submissive and subservient supporters. You have no other destiny." It is but natural some Conservatives and all good Liberals should resent this degrada- tion, for such it undoubtedly is, and there will be occasional scenes and revolts until an amendment arrives. The use of the closure whenever discus- sion seems to be unduly ince% venient and pro- longed puts a great power in the hands of Ministers in other ways, and it is freely, often despotically, exercised. Indeed, scorn for the House of Commons is the one distinguishing mark of the present Government, and it seems likely to last until the country has changed its mind upon many things and feels disposed to reassert its right to be treated ii a better fashion The rebellion of Monday was a flash indicative of what is coming. and there will be other flashes un- less the Government mends its ways.</p>
        <p>Colonel Pilkington has been looming large in the public eye once more, and this time in the House of Commons, where he spoke at some length on Wednesday with regard to the need for legislation ,in licensing matters. The member for Newton's views on this question sewn to be Sounder than those he expressed a few days ago about the war, and his remarks attracted some attention. We must compliment Colonel Pilkington, though on the Government side, for his stand against the cold-shouldering of temperance legislation by the speech from the Throne. He in— sists upon the licensing authorities having full power over licensing matters and over all licensed premises where alcoholic liquor is sold, and holds to the opinion that every licence should be under the same authority on the same conditions. It is doubtful whether a solution of the drink problem lies in these proposals,but they would certainly be advantageous in many respects. Colonel Pilkington is earnest in his endeavours to procure temperance legislation and we hope he will persevere id his endeavours to persuade the Government to introduce their Bill. We agree with him that the time is ripe for " good instalment of licensing reform," and like the child and the plum, we await it with open mouths.</p>
        <p>The editor of one of the most distiLguished magazines of the day has been heard complaining of the pessimism of modern writers. He finds the world full of clever people who can write— possibly, if his is the usual experience of magazine editors, and if he is really conscientious in his work of selection, much too full—but there are very few indeed who ever will write like a man who can sometimes, if rarely, be happy. Tragic little masterpieces—" little,' presumably, mess" ured by the amount of paper they cover, and not in literary quality—he can get in plenty; he only wants to meet a few writers who have brains, and yet do not consider this world the worst of all possible worlds, and suicide the only proper course for a sensible man to adopt. After all, however, this is only the sort of thing which has been said from the very beginning. No doubt Homer was remon- strated with for the sadness of many of his incid- ents; in the Latin poets certainly the soft melan- choly is a great charm. There is a fallacy in supposing that the happy wish always for literature bubbling over with the exuberance of the joy of life. The "luxury of woe" is not wholly a senti- mental idea. What could be sadder than the choruses of "Atalanta in Calydon," and yet, as a recent critic of Swinburne'has remarked, they have just the rhythm and sonority which makes the repetition an enjoyment to a man swinging along a country lane, his pulse tingling with the very pleasure of being. How could the little things of existence which give happiness inspire such music? It is surely a libel on art or literature to say that it is pessimistic, when out of the very sadness of things it makes that which is a joy tor ever.</p>
        <p>What are the arms of Wales P The subject is of some little importance, in consequence of the Welsh demand that their arms should be added to the Royal Standard. Obviously, it is first of all necessary to ascertain what those arms are. The English heralds state, more or less officially, that if Wales can boast of arms at all they are " Quarterly azure and gules, four lions passant gardant counter- charged ; but tipsily historical experts are strong- ly in favour of the dragon. There is authority for it at least as far back as the aixth century. There is no doubt that at one time the Welsh kings had the lion as their emblems and that the dragon then passed to the Saxons of 'Wessex, as more than one hillside inscription or rude cutting remains to show; but the Welsh subsequently reverted to the older use, and when Owen Glendower, army.., the dragonA wthe great-grandson of Llewellyn and the last crowned Prince of WAles, rose in arms against the reoenrerYhHilV s similar flag f standardßritain Henryenry VII., who appealed spoof- aswluaal authority h bornecotaor ri rtlyiVe edobl f lifeosebbmf en by claiming to be the heir of King Arthur of Cadwallon, on his landing at Milford Haven and his march to meet Richard 111. on the battlefield of Bosworth. It is true the dragon fall afterwards into disgrace, becoming an emblem of oppression and evil, and of all that true knights are bound to stamp out of existence, but this is no more a matter to be considered in such au inquiry than the further disrepute which has fallen on the unhappy creature by its Chinese asso- ciations. The point is solely one of historical accuracy, and the arguments for the dragon seem at least as cogent as those for the lion.</p>
        <p>The inhabitants of Stockton Heath have been called together again to pronounce an opinion upon the Warrington and. Northwich Light Railway scheme and their opinion is even more decisive against it than on a former occasion. At the meeting last week not a single hand was held up in opposition to a rcsolution approving of the action of the Parish Council in opposing the scheme. There are many features in connection with it which make it peculiarly obnoxious to the inhabitants of a residential district such as Stock- ton Heath undoubtedly is. The advantages to be derived are very problematical whilst the dangers and annoyances are obvious, even though they be reduced to the minimum. The scheme practically involves the appropriation of a consid- erable portion of the main thoroughfare for the purposes of a railway which may be without any - "light" attributes at all. When it is proposed to use the line for the transit of an ordinary railw- goods waggon its "light" features are not clear. Then again, there is still some do, whether the syndicate which is promoting it, have a terminus at Stockton Heath or whether it is intended to approach the Corporation with a view to allowing them to forward their trucks over the municipal tram-lines to a railway</p>
        <p>very</p>
        <p>mb-t</p>
        <p>will</p>
        <p>station.</p>
