      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>Either proposal is open to serious objection. A terminus at Stockton Heath would in all likelihood result in the blocking of the highway With goods traffic, whilst the;proposal to use the Corporation tram lines for the conveyance of goods waggons would be a distinct innovation and Would be strongly resented by the ratepayers. Much of the scheme is still "in the air" as the Earl of Jersey characterised the first application, but if the features already alluded to are correct, there is a great deal to which objection must rightly be taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>The Bill for Legalising Marriage with a deceased Wife's Sister is among those that have secured a favourable place for the present Session,and already Ita chances of passing, and its merits, are being Largely discussed. The Marriage Law Reform Association, instituted so long ago as the 15th Janu- ary, 1851, has, of course, considered every indivi- dual point bearing on the question For certain reasons,there had been a compromise about the year 1835, that "such marriages for the future should be absolutely avoided." Since 1851 the bill for legalis- ing such marriages has passed the House of Corn- raons on many occasions, but has met with effectual apposition in the House of Lords. Its promoters are very sanguioe, however, that it will become law Very soon Hope A this year revived, somehow, because when the present King was Prince of Wales, he and other members of the Royal Family have proved warm and constant supporters of the Measure. Whether under the new order of things the House of Lordi will be induced to relent, pro- riding the Bill passes the Lower House, is a matter put now of lively speculation. Sir William Gurdon 88 charge of the Bill.</p>
        <p>The Pure Beer Bill has also found a place for this E3ession, and will be introduced by Mr Purvis Member for Peterborough, It has been recalled, as an interesting circumstance, that fifty years ago there was an allegation that the bittering of ales W.as brought about by the use of strychnine, but the allegation was not sustained. It does not appear to ,be hinted now that, apart from the bleaching of 40P8, there is any cause for suspecting the bittering Ill_aterials used to be of a poisonous character. A Or respondent suggests that if the malt tax were Aewed it would be likely to ensure the purity of I;yer. The present beer duty he considers unfair,as Ab Rives foreign barley an advantage over British. Measured bushel of barley malt is heavier and Produces more beer than one of foreign. Apparently the best way to obtain pure beer and help British barley growers without injury to the consumers of beer, would be to repeal the present beer duty and raise revenue from beer by a malt tax, leviel ac- cording to the bulk, not the weight, of the barley malt used.</p>
        <p>Farmers who devote attention to the rearing of horses should take notice that there is likely to be a large and continuous demand for them for war Purposes. The chief difficulty hat lies in the way is the fact that no one seems to have any very clear idea as to the kind of horse that will best meet the approval of the authorities ; and in this case mm- hers of Parliament for the districts concerned would do a distinct service to their constituencies if they W9re to obtain and circulate particulars. Other- wise much money which might just as well go into the pockets of our agricultural population will find it way elsewhere.</p>
        <p>--- Who among us ever thought on Tuesday that it waB 44 pancake day." Truly the times seem out of Vnt and the old order changeth in many ways. n.rove Tuesday observances appear to be going With those of St• Valentine's Day, and that very raPidly.</p>
        <p>t Many merchants, and all the tobacco manufac- tv,urers, are already making 'insurances "against .ue risk of the Chancellor of the Exchequer increas- -IPR the duty on their particular class ot goods. So ar there does not appear to have been any kind of tall,„ le, but Messrs W D and II 0 Wills, Limited, tOtal.are said to have beaten their own record,and t,Vecord of the customs, by sending in a cheque latter for a quarter of a million sterling ! week they paid .070,000 out of a total collec- re,,',°f £400,000, and in one week the collector has th-PTed at Bristol something approaching double average yearly collection at Southampton.</p>
        <p>When clergym,in begin making use of their Parish magazines to deprecate juvenile soooking and the prevalence of the cigarette, there is room for both hope and expectation that practical results Will follow, This is the course that at least one. clergyman has adopted, and it encourages a belief that others will follow the example set before them. It the clergy and ministers of all denominations Would act in the same way, they might, with the Weight of wholesome public opinion at their backs, ,ale very near to puttiag an end to what s —,o%eacly recognised i as a crying evil of the time To lew 'peop!e the matter may appear trival, 'ft; " is in reality very serious, and one feels con- relied ced that public ooinion in regard to it might be IL., hat if once set in motion. So long as pub- 'eeling remains supine, however, nothing will or aa be done. It is too late in the day to enlarge Upon the prevalence ef the habit of smoking by A°Ye• The evil is admitted ; it is notorious. Bop Nio riot any longer take the trouble to smoke on the _8137 they do it openly, It is becoming a fixed and tolerated habit in our midst. Nor is it necessary to Say. 4. much of the injurious effect of smoking on bays. Would be absurd, indeed, to invoke medical testimony in support of this. What is needed is a ,:(.I.tanion and united impulse of public opinion .4e,thasters of religion, schoolmasters, and parents Periaselves may do much in warning and persuad- 'Oß the boys against cultivating the habit, and, where necessary, prohibition might be insisted up- 4_°/1. Anyhow, it is time the matter was taken in .",end more s:iri-usly and earnestly than has been the case hitherto.</p>
        <p>„.The hope that the movements which Lord Aitchener and his troops are engaged in at Present will end the war is cherished by the Public, whether as humanitarians or as tax- PaYers. The rumours that have prevailed of the thcoming Budget containing an eighteen-penny 41corne-tax proposal are enough to startle the most Tulegmatic, and, vast as is the wealth of the coon- the ontlo4k is certainly causing anxiety from Zhe financial as wail as other points of view. When Operations of the Government demand that two kir a half millions every week, all the year round, .4 all be raised by taxation, the withdrawal of such from the pockets of the people cannot but trlturiously affect trade and commerce. A reduction i azation is, of course, out of the question, but t spZild be possible to make the payment easier by r„."ding it over a greater number of years than is w"sent contemplated.</p>
        <p>It the 9 not very agreeable to find that, according to ,Qt turns of the Labour Department of the Board tta rade, employment in many important groups of An(ile,B continued to decline during the past month, nas been considerably worse than a year ago. glair, the first tim,l since November, 1897, the net re- the changes in 7ages recorded during the Fr 0° h was a decline, mainly due to the fall in the "' and steel trades. moamonimmimmo•</p>
