      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PREFERMENT OF A RUNCoRN POsT OFFICE OFFICIAL.</p>
        <p>Our congratulations are offered to Mr. 0 Christie, second clerk at the Runcorn Post Office, on his ap- politment as postmaster of Littleworth,near Rugby. Mr. Christie has spent almost the whole of his life in postal work, and for the last twenty years has been at Runcorn. where he is held in universal esti- mation both for his exemplary official life, and private straightforwardness. Mr, Christie's pro- motion is well deserved.</p>
        <p>Sr JOIIN AMBULANCE ASSOCIATION, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>BRANcff.</p>
        <p>At the examination in connection with the above association, recently held in the Technical Institute and conducted by Dr A Price, of Aintree, Liverpool, the following gained certificates qualifying them for membership of the St John Ambulance Brigade :—Messrs W Ashbrook, It Brooker, T Car- son, W Ford, R Gibbs, J Hargreaves, J Harrison,R L Hughes, J Leach, T Lunt, T Murphy, J Riwlinson, A Shaw, G Walton, J T Walker, and PC Gilpin.</p>
        <p>MER.S. ET MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a novel entertainment was given at the Seaman's Institute, under the title of the " Flower Queen's Court and Party." The programme was in the hands of a number of girls, attired in brilliant costumes, and interspersed with songs, recitations, gramaphone selections, &amp;c., the proceedings were at once striking and picture,que. The characters were represented as follows : Flower Queen, Miss Willcock,of Widnes ; primroses, Misses A Jones and li E Moore; daisies, E C Tonks and M Hawthorn; roses, S Atkin and E Summer- field; pansies, L Bishop anu J Bishop; lilies, A Coward and C Harrison; blue-bells, A Shaw and E Shaw; crown and cushion bearers, L Coward and E Hopwood; train bearers, H Hawthorn and D Shaw. An interesting item was the famous sketch "Briton and Boer." Mr. W. Grounds, as the accompanist, contributed in no small degree to the success of the entertainment, which was received with a continu- ous stream of applause. 'WIDNES AND, RUNCORN TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. On Friday, at the Widnes Town Hall, Mr It II Bickell, l lust, CE, an inspector of the Local Government Board, held an inquiry into the ap- plication of the Widnes Town Council for sanction to borrow £25,000 for the purposes of the Runcorn and Widnes Bridge Act. There were present besides the inspector,the Mayor (Alderman G I Neil),Alder- men Wareing and Sadler; Councillors S Owen, C Hemingway, ft Allen, and T Gerrard ; Messrs H S Oppenheim (town clerk), J S Sinclair, (borough surveyor), S Hoyle (borough accountant), T Craik (deputy town clerk) ; Messrs. II Cottrell (manager of the Liverpool Overhead Railway) J Webster (engineer of the scheme), W Handley (chairman of ' the Runcorn Urban District Council), i Imison,J Wilson, T Woods, F A Lake (law clerk to the Urban Council), E Marshall (secretary), J Wilding (surveyor), T Woods, and F J Poole. There was no opposition. Inquiries were invited, but as none were forthcoming the inquiry was closed, the Inspector remarking that he thought he might confidently say that the 30 years would be allowed for Runcorn. NULL-BROW RAGGED SCHOOL MISSION, RUNCORN. On Saturday, in connection with the above mis- sion, a very pleasant evening was spent under the presidency of the Rev J Poole in the Trinity Schools. "The Village Blacksmith" was capitally snug by Mr J Nall, who sang as a recall the "Old folks at home." Mr J Austin was recalled for "The Dublin Fusiliers," singing as the encore the comic song "Trouble," Miss Annett's songs entitled "Three old Maids of Lee" and "Footsteps in the Snow," were highly appreciated, and she fully deserved the ap- plause which greeted her efforts. "Our Jack's come home to-day" was sung in true nautical style by Mr Taylor, while Hr J FL Harrison for the old song "Father O'Flynn" received quite an ovation. "Darby and Joan" was feelingly rendered by Miss Turner Mr A Grounds brought a most enjoyable evening to a close by giving the humorous sketches entitled "Hey up !its coming"and "I must have a day off for that." Mr B Johnson contributed to the success of the entertainment by officiating as pianist. During the interval tea, coffee and buns were dispensed by Mesdames A Lightburn, W Collier, the Misses Andrews and Fisher, and others. WIDNEs Woneriesie's CATHOLIC BILL. On Monday the Widnes Workmen's Catholic ball was held In the Drill Hall. The room had been prettily decorated, long lines of streamers and bannerettes being tastefully interwoven with gar- lands of flowers which were suspended from the roof and festooned round the sides of the building. Mr Fred Bishop, in company with the committee, had had the work in hand, and a very artistic finish ' they had imparted to the whole of the decorations, Dancing commenced at 9.15 and was kept up until a late hour. The Mayor (Alderman G I Neil) should have opened the ball, but owing to a sudden and severe indisposition he :was :unable' to fulfil his engagement. Owing to the energy of the M C.'s, Messrs S Flannacran, P Manning, W Hooker, E Brennon, and T Fishwick, there Was no lack of partners for the various dances. The funds will be devoted to S'6 Patrick's and St Mary's Parish. A good sum was realized, and the secretary. Mr T Gerrard, is to be 'Congratulated on the all round success of the enterprise. The refreshments were provided by Mr T Laws. A WESTON LABOURER FOUND DROWNED, Coroner R. Dobson, held an inquiry at the Rnyal Oak, Weston, on Tuesday afternoon,which disclosedl how George Routledge, a Chemical labourer, aged about 55 years. of Company's Row, Weston, was found drowned. Routledge left home, it appears, about 5 o'clock on Sunday afternoon to go to his usual night shift at the Weston Works, where he worked in company with two other men named Sutton and Rawbotham. About 11 o'clock, having finished What he had to do, he repaired to:a hut set apart for the men to partake of their meals in. Here he lay himself down on a form. Between 3 and 4 o'clock Rawbotham and Sutton also went to the hut for a rest and something to eat. Upon their advent however, Routledge got up and went out, leaving his coat behind. He did lilt return again. Next morning his companion took up his bag to return to his home, when they found that Routledge had left his supper untouched. Sub- sequently they reported the matter to Constable Wylie. lnqniriesinetituted disclosed the fact, that Routledge's next door neighbour had seen him go in the direction of the old soapery. He snoke to him and Routledge complained that his wife was not satisfied with some potatoes and things he had got for her, and he passed on muttering that" some- thing would have to be done." This led to Con- stables Wylie and Rowlands dragging the Weaver Canal and about 3 o'clock on Monday afternoon the holy was recovered. There were no marks of vio- lence, and only 3-134 in cash on deceased when he was picked up, and as there was nothing to indi- cate how he got into the water the jury returned an nom verdict.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREDI</p>
        <p>CRACKERS, CRISP AND</p>
        <p>'MEANLY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>SALAIII CAPTURE. Erie Scheiling, a sailor, hailing from Guernsey, is to be brought up before the Runcorn magistrates this morning,on a charge of stealing 36 cigars from the Wheat Sheaf Inn,Bridge-street. It appears that prisoner was apprehended by Police Sergeant Samuel Gregory in Percival-lane with the cigarA on his person, shortly after information of their losS being given to Gregory. AFTER MANY DAYS!</p>
        <p>Thomas Clarke, labourer of Norley, was appre- hended on Thursday, by P C Wilding at Runcorn, under a warrant issued in August 1900, for in- decently a§saulting a little girl at Norley.—Tne Oakmere police anchorites have been communicated with and will remove him in due course from Run- corn police station where he is now confined. HALTON WESLEY GUILD. Last Monday evening the usual meeting of the above was presided over by Mr W Rowe, there being a fair attendance. The lecturer was Mr Albert Rowe, and his subject : " Fractures, and the practical way of dealing with them " . The lecturer dealt with the subject from the St John Ambulance standpoint,and was assisted by Mr Peter Rowlinson The lecture was much appreciated. HOLY TRINITY SCHOOLS, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Last Friday evening the prize; awarded for regu- lar attendance, &amp;c., to the scholars of Trinity Day Schools were distributed by Mrs Killick. The Rev C It Killick (vicar) presided,and there were present in addition to Mrs Killick, the Rev N J Poole, Messrs John Littler. W J Cunningham, J llealkeld, Branch, T II Annett (headmaster), and others. There was a large attendance of children, parents and friends. The prize list is as follows :—Boys,— Standard VII.: H Fearnett. Standard VI.: A Ellis, Standard V.: E France, T Hewitt. Stan- dard IV. : J Skillicorn, J Ormes. Standard III.: J Harvey, A Hayes, iV Skillicorn, A Smith. .1 Street. Standard IL ; J Banner, F Hurst, A Johnson, D Wheeler. Standard I. L Ditchfield, H Forshaw; C Hewitt; E Jones, It Russell, J Parker, A Moulsdale, H Hayes, C Cresswell, J Ellie, P Orme.% G Ellis. Girls. —Standard V ; G Hill. Standard IV :E Annett, H Ellis, M Fearnett, A France, F Harrison. Standard 111 : E Forshaw. Standard II M Brown, A Ellis, A Fryer, M Gillgrass, E Moqerstted, N Wright. Standard I; E Jones, E Crefswell, and M Jackson. During intervals Mr M Branch entertained the gathering with the "Tin Gee-Gee," and the "Powder Monkey," the latter being sung in response to an encore. Miss Hicks contributed two solos on the pianoforte, and the children gave a couple of songs, items which contributed a great deal to the pleasure of the evening. The meeting closed with , the usual votes of thanks. A WIDNES MAN IN TROUBLE AT CHESTER. ' At the Chester City Police Court, on Saturday, before Messrs. J J Cunnah and Roger Jackson, William Fleming, 11, Latham-street, Widnes, was summoned by the London and North-Western Railway Company for travelling on the company's railway without having previously paid his fare,and with intent to avoid payment,and also with travell- ing from Mostyn to Chester and giving a false name —Mr. Fenna pro•iecuted, and stated that defendant at the time of the offence was in the employ of Messrs Pooley, weighing machine contractors forl the company, and on the 19th January he arrived at Sandycroft Station at 1.15 p.m He was asked for his ticket, and replied " Puss." The station- master asked to be allowed to see it, but the defen- dant after searching his pockets said he had not got one. He then told the stationmaster that he worked for Messrs. Pooley, repairing machines, and had been working at Mostyn, and gave his name as Rowland Pritchard. As a matter of fact the defendant was not employed by the firm on railway work, and had been on this day sent to work at a colliery, and his fare had been paid by the firm. Messrs fooley had assisted the company in every way in the matter, and when they heard of the offenee they dismissed Fleming from their employ. —Evidence was given in support of this statement and the defendant, who pleaded guilty to the charge, was fined 103 and costs on each charge. Pearl Life Assurance Co , Ltd. The annual report of this interesting company was presented at the thirty-seventh Annual General Meeting of the Company, held at the Company's Offices, London Bridge, City, 8.C., on Friday last. From it we glean that the company is in a sound and reliable financial condition; and that the pro- gress of the company during the twelve month just concluded was of a very solid character. The quinquennial valuation, which has just been com- pleted by Mr It Al Moore, A I A, up to December last shows a surplus of £203,588, which is a very interesting figure, and ons that should fill the general public with confidence in the Company. 0 that amount, too, the directors recommended th division of £30,093 amongst participating policy - holders, and the balance of £172 595 has been catried forward. As showing the extended work of the society, the total income last year was £882,622 17s 6d, being an increase of £118,360 2s 54 over that of the previous year. The company has paid 48.424 life claims and matured endowments during the year, which with bonuses amounted to £304,370 123 54. And since its establishment the Company has paid in claims,Sce, no less a sum than £2,747,192 17s 3d. The assurance funds, with capital paid up, now amounts to .C1,200,08i 3s Id, showing an increase for the year of .C 201,973 17s 3d. A glance at the report, published in our advertising columns, should prove the stability of the company as an investment for workingmen. The Managing Director at the Chief Office, Adelaide Buildings, London Bridge, City, 8.C., or the District Super- intendent for Runcorn (Mr Joseph Makinaon, 9, Victoria-road), would only be too pleased to supply any information gratis.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, PRII)AY, FEBRUARY 22, )9 FREE CHURCHES UNITED MISSION. -- Encouraging Gathering. Very properly, on the eve of the great mission of the Free Churches, of which Warrington has heard so much lately a meeting of the local workers in connection with it was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Cburch to welcome the missioner—the Rev John Gould. This meeting was practically the last of the series of preparatory meetings whereby the Free Churches of Warrington have sought to prepare themselves for the work of the mission. The attend- ance was most encouraging and augured well for the success of the efforts about to be put forth dur- ing the mission. Two distinctive features mark- ing the unity of the Free Churches in the work were noticeable. In the rostrum were to be seen representatives of all the free churches in the town and in the gallery above was a large choir, the members of which had been drawn from the various Nonconformist choirs. Mr Gould comes to Warrington from Cambridge, where he has been the presiding genius of Methodism in East Anglia daring the past ten years. Prior to that, he was .actively connected with mission work in busy 'centres like Bolton, Hull and York. On his first .visit to Warrington a few months ago when he attended a reception at which many of the workers were present,he made a very favourable impression, and during the past week that trances:ion has been deepened. Saturday's Meeting of Workers. The Rev J YONGE, president of the Warrington and District Free Church Council, :onducted the meeting of workers on Saturday, The Rev J Gould, the missioner, had a cordial reception, and he was accompanied on the platform by representatives of all the Free Churches. The PRESIDENT, in the course of an introductory address, said they were met there that night 89 a com- pany of fellow-Christians who had made themselves interested in the great mission they were socking to in- augurate. It would be a thought of considerable interest and help to them to know that they were not the only body of Christian people who were met together that Inight under similar conditions, for he supposed it was correct to say that in a great number of towns and cities throughout this country, gatherings precisely similar to that were at that hour convened for the same purpose. It was really a very wonderful movement when they came to think of it. Great issues must come from it by the blessing and grace of God. It could not do less than 'quicken the churches with new life and larger faith and deeper love and holier zeal. Beyond the (picks: ening of the churches this great united movement must surely tell upon the people at large,_becaume after all,the peo- ple. ti nlwat taht soanadsnocefovt reedani at l whichibyee un noi si t nu spectacle that was presented of churches of various de- gstiatntellthis one great move. cuhproinsttiahnoupgehotpf ple aeond- chriatian churches that they were divided and sslit up and held aloof one from the other, but if the National Federation of Free Churches accomplished nothing more than to have inaugurated this great nnitei simultaneous mission, and thereby giving a great object lesson of the power of Christian unity it would have accomplished a very blessed result. To his mind one of the most beautiful features of that united movement was the illustration it gave of chrietianity. It was their Lord's great prayer that all his disciples might be one and he (the President) hoped they were all doing their part to hasten on the accomp- lishment and fulfilment of that great desire. They had marched as separate regiments long enough. Now they had learned a larger wisdom, and they were marching not as brigades but as one great army of the living God. Of course there was still work for them to do as denomi- nationalists. They must be loyal to their own particular churches which they loved, but there was something they desired more, and there I was something they loved more deeply Still. Tnev ' Wanted their nation to be filled with a spirit :lithe gospel and their fellow-countrymen to be brought der the power of Jesus Christ. Do net let them for- get in their conduct of this mission and in their indivi- dual behaviour and deportment in it, that they were a united body and that therefore they had to give doe consideration to the various thoughts and feelings which might be represented. They did not want to conduct it on the lines of any one denomination ; they wished to conduct it on such lines that they would be perfectly happy together, that there should be no feeling of any kind but that of cordiality ; and that they should con- duct it in such a way as would tell upon the people of Warrington that of a truth the Lord was In their midst working in them and through them. They had reason to be very thankful that there had been found fer them a good and worthy brother to be the missioner, it the person of the Rev John Gould. He could assure the missioner that he could begin his work with the conviction that he had their entire confidence and affectionate sympathy. ill through the mission let them rally round hire, following his lead loyally and obediently. He supposed they had present the large majority of those friends who had been working as canvassers from house to house, and he trusted they would find a wondrous reward in the result of the mission. He hoped they would follow up the good work done by bringing people to the services. Mr S B STRINGER, as the convener of the canvassing committee presented a short report on the work which heel been done. The committee had worked harmon- iously together and something like 42,000 invitations had been given. The more the members of the free churches rubbed against other, the mare they liked each other, and appreciated more the points un which they were agreed. The Rev, C H ROMS remarked that as one of the joint secretaries, he should like to bear testimony to the fact that one special feature of encouragement had been the warm loyalty of all the fees churches of Warrington in that great Divine idea. It had brought them together Moaderfully and as they had (tome closer together they bad felt that there was not much difference between them after all. He had not met with a refusal of assist- ance and judging from the willingness of the workers it bad been a labour of love. • The Rev. Jorrta GOULD, who bad a cordial reception, said they had done him a very great honour in in- viting him tol be the misssioner. The ten days' public service would have a worthy result; of that he felt sure. ['hey were face to face with ten days of supreme moment. The preacher proceeded to appeal to the workers to make the mission a personal matter with themselves. They must make themselves responsible for at least one person. What neighbours had they no. ? Let Sunday school teachers begin in their class. They must cot be gingerly or too dandified to say that the salvation of any man was not their concern. One of the great services a mission performed for them was to do away with that modesty that led England people to come to the conclusion that e7ery man's religion and spiritual interests were his own business and that no other man had a right to meddle. He appealed to them to avail themselves of the oppor- hanky which the mission gave them. fie knew there was an objection to compel others to come into Christian fellowship lest after all they should not be able to stand. This had been urged upon him in connection with re- vival Missions. Mr Gould proceeded to show that their business as Christians was to compel strangers to come in and leave the results with God. lie gave testimony of the good results which had followed such compulsion during his own experiences as a missioner. In concln- Mott, Mr Gould mentioned the arrangements which had been made for the reception of inquiries during the mission. The Rev C H HOCKEN having made several announce- ments, the meeting terminated. Sunday's Meetings. The Mission commenced in real earnest on Sunday. In the afternoon a young people's service was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel, when one of the most Impressive rallies of Sunday school scholars in Warring- ton was witnessed., From every Nonconformist Sunday school, scholars over 12 years of age marched from their respective centres to the Mission. Every seat in the vast building was occupied, and the attendance was so great that many had to stand. The service was conduc- ted by the Misaioner, who based An earnest address on the words "My father thou shalt be the guide of mission f mmyilln bife.e" After referring to the Divine Fatherhood, he pointed out God's eJaiin upon them. Referring o h said such a national simultaneous mission throughout the length and breadth of the land had never before been witnessed in the history of their nation and it might be a long time before such an occasion would ammo asain. God bad called upon the Free Churches to unie in their councils and lay upon their hearts the salvaties of Eng- land As the result of the mission he believed there would be a great ingathering of souls. He appealed to his hearers to lead the way in this great work for the Master. The vast corigregatiirrs joined in singing San. key's hymns, a united choir under the conductorship of Mr Walter Brough, greatly adding to the effectivenees of the singing. Commenting at eight o'clock, an evangelistic service was held in the same building when there was a large congregation. The missioner was accompanied on the rostrum by the-Revs T Bishop (80ld-street Wesleyan), C II Hocken (Bewsey-road Wesleyan). John Forage (Wycliffe Congregational). C Finch (Leghsstreet Primi- tive Methodist). J T Schofield (Golberne-street Baptist), J F Matthews (Latchford Baptist), J S Hughes (Hill Cliffe Baptist), and E T Reed f (Emmanuel (Reformed Church.) A powerful gospel address was delived by the missioner, and at the close he appealed to his hearers to openly acknowledge Jesus Christ. An inquiry meeting was subsequently held at which an encouraging number of inquirers were present. Widnes. On Saturday the Widnes Free Church Council com- menced the simultaneous mission which is to be held all over England. Special services were held in the Milton</p>
        <p>Congregational Church and the Hartland-street Wesleyan Chapel. In the termer place of worship the Rev G Brain, South Wales, officiated and in the latter the Rev JEI Cartwright, of Seaforth. For some time missioners and mission bands have been holding open-air meetings with the view of inviting the people to attend the ser- vices, and the fruits of the street preaching were seen in the unusually large congregations which gathered at the appointed sacred edifices. Already there are indi- cations that the mission will be a unique and a thorough success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORR]SPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>As an indication of the enjoyment Runcorn Heath ono Common will afford, when fully trans- formed into a pleasure ground, I have but to quote the praise of the crowd of skaters who on Friday last, availed themselves of an exhilarating spin upon the waters of the splendid lake already completed there. True the ice was thin, and a few fell in ; but, no danger, further than a wetting of the lower extremities was risked, and who of us would not risk that much in pursuit of our winter sport ? A matter of importance for the District Council to consider arises out of our little experience, how- ever, and in their code of regulations they should make some provision which will prevent the lake being monopolised by "hockey teams" and forbid it altogether rather than allow the generality of the public to have their limbs endangered simply for the amusement of a "rough element."</p>
        <p>The Transporter Bridge inquiry is now a thing of the past, and from the general feeling and ex- pression of the district, together with the indica- tion of the inspector" conducting the inquiry, there is little to fear that the Local Government Board will put any obstacle in Runcorn's way.</p>
        <p>So far as can be learnt, there is to be no contest for the local seats on the Cheshire County Council, and this afternoon nominations will be handed in to Mr Ashton (the deputy returning officer) of Nlessrs A R Norman and E Imison, the returning Councillors. At one time there was some talk of opposing Mr Poison, but so far, the Liberals of the district have been more or less persuaded to take his long and useful services into consideration, and allow him to resume his seat 'unopposed The pro- cedure decided upon seems to be to nominate both canditates for both seats, and let them choose as to which district they will it for. As the candidates are both eminently fitted for the post, it is a matter of little importance to either Northerners or Southerners who represent them. Finally, the Dis- trict is to be congratulated on being so ably repre- sented without the expense of a contest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>POOTBLLL.</head>
        <p>POOTBLLL.</p>
        <p>Runcorn whacked St. Helens right enough on Saturday, but the form displayed was nothing great, and the only thing it indicates is that the match at Oldham (to-morrow) on Saturday,will not be a very brilliant one. Still with the advent of Butterworth and Jones and a general " bucking up" all round, a different aspect could be put on affairs, and Runcorn 8 chances very much enhanced. For all that I predict an unqualified drubbing of my own favourites.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLllSES.—According to" Mer- cantile Gaz-tte," the number of New Bankruptcy Pro- ceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending February 16th, was 85. The number in the corresponding sa eek of last year was 81, showing an increase of 4, being a net decrease in 1901, to date,of 32' DRLICIOUS HAZILWATTBB TEL DELICIOUS MA.ZAWATTES TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEDI TEA I SOE AGENTS: H&amp;NRY MILLING AND CO., LTD. WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5, HORHOLARKET-IHR&amp;ET, WARIIINGTMIL</p>
        <p>TO CURE A COLD IN ON DAY Take Laxative litromo Quinine Tablets. All Druggists re- nd the money if it fails to cure. 1/1/. The genuine is amped L. B. Q.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Its Progress at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Its Progress at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The simultaneous mission commenced at Runcorn, on Saturday evening last, with a special meeting for the workers at St. Petal's Church. The Missioner (the Rev. A Wells) was present, and also the other Free Church Ministers of the town, The gathering proved one of special comfort and blessedness, and all present partook in the administration of the Lord's Supper. Prayer for the success of the mission was offered, and a keynote of earnestness was struck, which we trust inspired the workers with zeal sufficient to conduct a fruitful cam- paign' On Snaday,special sermons were preached throughout the town. At de. Paul's Church, the Rev. S Wright occupied the pulpit in the morning, and preached a telling and effective sermon.—ln the afternoon there was a "Young people's service." which was largely attended by the SuLday School Scholars from the various local free churchs. The ministers present included : Revs. A Wells (missioner). S Wright, S Morris, Osborne, R Morrison, J B Foster, and W A Eyre, all of whom partook in the inspiring service. There were a number of conversions, and a good work accomplished. The Sunday evening service, at St. Paul's, was con- ducted by the Rev A Wells, and was also largely attended. Taking for his text " And the God in whose hand thy breath is, and whose are all thy ways, has theta not glorified," Daniel v. 23. He sketched the feast of Belshazza, and illustrated how the King was in the midst of his merrymaking and banqueting when the fingers of a man's hand wrote on the wall the prophecy of his downfall. All Scripture was given by inspiration. , and all the circumstances recorded in the life of indi- viduals were designed by God to be either a warning or an encouragement to them. He might even be speaking to some against whom the charge, or at least a similar one, might be brought as was brought against the Chaldean King. They could all with profit review the charge ; and look at its m ning, so that they could i apply themsely -s to t. It was clearly a charge of neglecting tie glorify God. There were two ways of glorifyieg Gad—by worship and by service. EiCh one of them had a mission or purpose in life ; and, God had appointed a certain plan or course, and given them faculties and powers with the express ' object of enabling them to fulfil that purpeie. Belshazza had failed to use his opportunities to serve God, and so failel to glorify Him. Whatever their position in life, each was responsible for certain duties which had been laid upon them. If they were neglected Clod failed to be glorified. Every act might be said to be a hymn of praise; their whole life might he described as worship, and God required them to be diligent in business as well as feryeat in spirit. Gol had given them that mysterious something called life, or nervous energy, which no one could tell what it was. It was awful to think that men and wornee should spend the strength they had reeerved in blasphemy. Profanity rang au on the air in the to gn of Runcorn. As he walked down the street on the previous night he , shivered again as he passed ranks of men and boys com- ing from the football field, and he was sorry that one of his friends in buttons had not been by his side. He thanked God that they did punish them in Runcorn. But God heard every word uttered. Every thought was recorded, and the time would come when they would have to face it. Tney might fancy God had forgotten, but the punishment of the wicked would surely come. They might forget because of earthly prosperity or phy- sical strength, but these things were as the morning cloud or early dew when God spoke. On Monday afternoon, a special service was held, ex- clusively for women, at Brunswick Schoolroom, and was attended with a considerable measure of success. The noon-day prayer meetings, the afternoon Bible readings, in the morning chapel at St Paul's, have also been fruitful from a mission standpoint. Toe ministers officiated at these gatherings in rotation. Throughout the week services have been also held at St Paul's Chapel, each evening at 7.13, being conducted by the missioner. The singing has been of an inspiring character, and great praise is due to the united choir for the very capable aid they have given in this direction. Several =aversions have been made, and the collec- tions towards the expenses of the mission have been highly satisfactory. On Sunday next there will be a further taterchange of pulpits and special meeting for men only, at Bethesda church, in the afternoon, at 2.30. The prayer meetings and bible readings will also continue up to Tuesday the 26th, as will also the week night services. FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>SUPPER AT THE PARIss Roo.—Oa Saturday evening an excellent supper was generously provided in the Parish Room, Frodsham, by Miss Ashley, for the three adult classes of the Overton Sunday School, in the mem- bers of which she has always taken a lively interest. About forty scholars ware present. Subsequently a pleasant evening x as spent in gam's of various kinds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>......I,,ml46lwwWlM</head>
        <p>......I,,ml46lwwWlM</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom Boothroyd's Pupils' Concert. Once again, Mr Torn Boothroyd delighted an audience in the Assembly Room of the Conserva- tive Club on Monday evening, with an excellent concert, given for the most part by his pupils. The banjo, mandoline and guitar orchestra, com- posed entirely of pupils of Mr Tom Boothroyd, contributed a number of items in a manner which reflected the greatest credit upon the conductor. The selections included "Poet and Peasant" (Suppe), " Starlight " (Ellis), "Belle of Scarboro' " (Turner). " Fantasia on Scotch Melodies" (Hucke), and Beni Rubati" (Francis). The last named piece was given by special request. some exceedingly sweet music was extracted from the various string instruments, and the audience showed their appreciation by repeated applause. Mr Tom Boothroyd weilded the baton, and the excellence of the performance was evidence of his careful training as tutor. The orchestra was composed of the following :—First mandolines, Miss Nolland (leader), Miss A Smith, Miss F Jordon, Miss I Brootnhead, Miss E Wilcox, Miss E Moss; second mandolines, Miss S Lathes,' , Miss M Warren; violins, Miss M Cheswortb, Mr H Butcher; mandola, Miss PCheswerth; baojeaurines, Miss E Grimshavv, Mr C E Wilcox; piccolo banjo, Mr G Kendrick ; first banjos, Mr H Sinclair, Mr A Cotterill ; second banjos, Miss A Warren, Miss MI White ; guitar, Miss B Simpson: bass guitar, Mr I P Sinclar; effects. Miss M Warren. Apart from the concerted efforts of the orchestra, several of the members gave individual items of a very promising' character. Miss Leonora Nolland's mandoline solos were a musical treat, and she thoroughly maintained the high reputation she has gained as a capable mandolinist. Miss A Smith is another player of great promise, and her efficient treatment of the solo " Berceuse " (Evans) won for her a hearty round of applanse. Miss Mabel White was accompanied by Mr Boothroyd in her contribution on the banjo, and she gave a faithful interpretation, of Folkestone's " Merry-go-round:" Mention must also be made of the violin solo by Mr Herbert Butcher, who was very successful in his 'treatment of Verdi's" La Traviata." A march by Heath was also well given by a banjo quartette party consist- of Messrs 11 Sinclair, A Cotterill, G Kendrick and T Boothroyd. The programme would have been in- complete without a violin solo by the conductor of the orchestra. Mr Boothroyd was perhaps never heard to greater advantage than on Monday even- ing when he essayed "The Broken Melody," by Auguste Van Biene• The solo was given with an exactness and delicate touch which gained for Mr Boothroyd unstinted applause. The special artistes engaged were Miss Beatty, soprano; Mr W H Ire- land, tenor; and Mr Whit Cunliffa, humorist. Miss Beatty's two songs werel"When the heart is young' and "Scenes that are brightest," in both of which she was heard to advantage. Mr Wll Ireland• 4 pure-toned tenor voice was well suited to the songs "Good night beloved" and 'Mary,' the last named being e•Tecially well sung, Mr Whit CaWiffe is an accomplished humorist, and gave an entertainment far above the average. His mimicry and humorous , sketches Were so much appreciated as to win for him encore after encore. The accompaniments during the evening were efficiently played by Mr E Wilkes A.L.C.M. Warrington Cycling Club. The annual rnec.itig of the above club was ;held 11. t the Patten Arms Hotel last week, Mr C W Wood 'n the chair. There were about 40 members present. Mr. E Pickavance, junr., presented the annual report, which was unanimously adopted. The following officers for 1901 were appointed :—Presi- dent, Mr John Naylor; captain, Mr b Davies; sub- Captain, Mr J Newton; hon. treasurer, Mr W Mounfield; financial bon, Sec., Mr A BAoth; general hon sec., Mr E Pickavance, jun.; committee, Messrs W Litton, W H Bellian, UII Smith, W Brown, F Barber, E 'Clare, and Dr. Kishy ; hon. auditors, Messrs A Clive and F G;rnett; N.C.U. delegates, Messrs E Pickavance, jun., andG H Smith.</p>
        <p>• naLictous gAZAWATVIR IRA. DRLICIOITS MAZAVVATTRII TA • DELICIOUS 31 IaktVA:CTEETR &amp;vac AGNICTS : ITIMZY NULLING Co.. LTD. witrlLeLg Azin nvrAIL GIVICICREI ETC., 5, 110118EbrktliCKT-8111EXT, WAHROGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE Cesns.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court, Newton, before Mr Gillespie on Monday morning, John Broderick was charged with being drunk in Newton, on Saturday, and was fined 5s and costs.—James Rigg, for a like offence in Earlestown was fined 2s 61 and costa. T. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. _ _</p>
        <p>The fourth annual Shrove Tuesday social gather- ing in connection with the above church was halo in the Mission Hall, Earlestown, on Tuesday even- ing. About I`2o persons were present and ciancing was commenced at 8 o'clock, The M.C.'s were Messrs Spencer and Hardman. The proceeds were in aid of the Parochial funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>P.S.A.</head>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. Society was a meeting specially for women. Taere was a crowded attendance of ladies, over which Mrs Dave presided. An earnest address was given by Miss Allatt, and recitations of a suit- able character were submitted by Miss Newton. Vocal items were given. by Mrs Glover, and Miss Williamson officiated at the piano. The meeting was of a hearty and enthusiastic character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LOCAL MAINTENANCE CASE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MAINTENANCE CASE.</p>
        <p>At Altrincham Petty Sessions, on Monday, Mr Broadbent, relieving officer, summoned Samuel Hamnett, a farm bailiff residing at Hermitage-lane, Winwick, t.) snow cause why he should not cmtri- bute towards the maintenance of his father and mother, William and Sarah Ann Hamnett, of Timperley, who have become chargeable to the common fund of the Bucklow Union. Mr Broadbent said defendant's parents were 71 and 69 years of age and had been in receipt of 7s a week out-door relief. There were five sons, tour of whom were contribut- ing, but defendant was not. Defendant hid been written to several times, but had not replied, and they had to summon him.—An order was made for defendant to pay Is a week, and he had to pay the costs of the case. GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the C-i-operative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Mon- day evening. Bro Williams, C T presiding. After opening hymn and prayer the following items were given :—Piano solo, " Hilarity," Bro Willis; song, "The golden love," Mrs Williams ; Lancashire read- ing, " Heaw Billy Armatage managed to get a neet's lorlgin's," Mr Warburton; song, Miss Woods ; : song ',Killarney," Miss Thomas; recitation, "Story of a woman's revenge," Mr Alcock ; song, " A Sum- mer shower, "Mrs Williams ; recitation, " The three bidders," Miss Ord; auto harp sob, " Old folks at home," Miss Woods; the various inms were well received and appladded. Arrangements were thee made for the visit to the Wesley Hall Lodge Liver- pool, Sr PETER'S CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a parochial tea was held in the St Peter's Schools, Newton. There was a very fair number of parishioners present, and the tea which was excellently served was much enjoyed. An adjournment was afterwards made to the Town Hall where an entertainment was given, the first part of the programme being occupied by Mr Hutchinson and triend3 as " The Pierrots," but the selections submitted could hardly be considered appropriate to a church gathering. Some of • the items were Rood and were well rendered. The second part of the programme was taken up by members of the Church choir and was much enjoyed. The vocalists were Messrs Turner, Niather, Davereux and Dring, Bancks and Gee. Miss Holland,Miss Mather,and Miss Mary Blything gave piano solos, and Miss Mather and Mies Baybut con- tributed vocal items. On the motion of the Rev James Ryder, a vote of thanks was accorded the entertainers, and all who had worked in connection with the affair, WARGRAVE CHURCH, EIRLESTOWN. On Tuesday evening a tea party and concert in connection with the above church was held in the Wargrave Schools. An excellent tea was provided about 100 persons sitting down, their wants being attended to by lady members of the congregation. The concert which followed was of an enjoy- able character, the following programme being sub- mitted ;—Piano solo " Warblings at Eve." Miss Scriven ; Ripe." The Haven." choir; song, "Queen of Angels "Mr S Henderson; song, "In Mellow Autumntide," Miss Wright; quartette "Dream of Paradise," Misses Shepherd and Molyneux, Messrs Chandley and Melyneux ; song, "Tell me, swallow," Miss Corlett; glee, "The old brigade"; quartette, "Dear little Shamrock," Misses Wright and Moly- neux, Messrs Carson and Molvneux; song, "When the boys come home." Mr John Molyneux ; song, "The priceless gift." Miss Holt; song, "The devout lover," Mr John Molyneux ; violin solo, " Bone- dictus," Mr Peplow ; song, "Simon the Cellarer." Mr W Liptrott; quartette, "The Children's Home," 3lessrs Jones avid Mayne Henderson and Caron; glee," Daylight is fading." The Rev .T Carson pro- posed a vote of thanks to all who had assisted in getting up the tea and concert. The proceeds, he said, were to be devoted to the church renovation fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE .SOCIETY</head>
        <p>RARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE .SOCIETY</p>
        <p>The 161st quarterly meeting of this society was held in the Co-operative [fall on Monday evening. Mr. D Forrester presided and there was a fair attendance of members. The balance sheet and report stated that the cash receipts were no= 13s 6d, being an increase over last quarter, of 1.879 6s 613-cl and compared with the corresponding period of last year the increase was £1,609 Os 6d. The Com- mittee congratulated the members upon the quar- ters trade passing .£lO,OOO. The additional figure formed a bold landmark in the Society's useful and remarkably successful career. The net profits realised were £1.530 2s 71 to which they proposed adding £5 Os Lid, from Dividend Reserve fund and to pay a dividend of 3* in .£ on members pur- chases. During the quarter 50 new members had b.-en admitted, and 24 had withdrawn, leaving 1.615 on the register,of whom 1,194 had brought in checks. A contract had been placed for the build- ing of stabling. &amp;c. The necessary preliminary work in connection with the new branch shop and two blocks of cottages is well in hand and they hoped to have these several undertakings erected during the approaching building season. Mr D Forrester was re-elected president for another 12 months. Messrs. Swift and Jones were nominated for c-mimittee of management. Mr T Haselden was elected auditor. The report of the delegate to the wholesale quarterly meeting was received and adopted. A question of stock-taking on a uniform principle was introduced and discussed and the recommendation of the Committee was adopted.</p>
        <p>ST. 7LENS. _ HAYDOCK PAIIKISTEIPL EASES, MARCH IST AND 2ND.</p>
        <p>For the convenience of the racing public desirous —of journeying to this meeting the Great Central Railway Company announce in our advertising columns that they will rue through trains from St. Helena on each dav running dirnct to the Havdock Park (RaCehouse Station) which station adjoins the Course.</p>
        <p>A ST. ELENS JURYMAN'S APOLOGIES -.FINE RBIIITTED. _ _</p>
        <p>At St Helens Town Hall, on Wednesday, Daniel Lees, of Homer-street, who, last week, was fined £5 for re- fusing to attend and sit on the jury, appeared before the coroner, Ntr Brighouse, to apologise and to ask for a re- missien of the fine. He was ignorant on the matter, being no scholar, and no: having been on a jury before The Coroner: Do you dispute the fact that you are liabfe to serve? Lees: I admit it now from the infor- mation I have gathered since. After further discussion, the Coroner asked if he was prepared to say that be regretted that he did not obey the summons ? Lees said he was. The Coroner thereupon remitted the fine. It gave him no pleasure to fine anyone. He wanted to work in harmony with the jurors of South-West Lanca- shire, because it was go wide a district that punctuality must be observed by everyone in order that the work might be got through properly.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Thomas Sutton.</p>
        <p>It is with regret we announce the death of Mrs. Thomaq Sutton, wife of Alderman Sutton J.P which took place in a painfully sudden manner at her residence, 9.Springfield-street,on Tuesday after- noon a about one o'clock: The causes of death were influenza an! apoplexy. The deceased lady who wa.,..• 66 years of age, and leaves five sons, had been in a deiicate state of health for some time and had been attended by Dr. Adams. She had been on a visit. t Southport tor the benefit of her health, *o which place it had been her intention to return shortly. There was apparently on Tuesday morning lan improvement in her condition, but a few hours later she was :taken suddenly ill, and became un- closcions, and Alderman Sutton had only just resehed the house when she expired. Alderman Sutton and the family hrtve received many expres- ona of sympathy in their loss. DELicioUs MAZ&amp;WAIV. TRA DELICIOUS MA Z AWATTES TEA DELICIOUS NrAzAWAiE TEA. SoLicaoater, : HENRY adir.LING AND co . LTD* WHOLERLLE AND RETAIL GROCERS lac., 5. ZIOESEMIEZET STEET, wAiUXNeTott.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHESHIRE COUNTY</head>
        <p>THE CHESHIRE COUNTY</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>No Contests in the Locality.</p>
        <p>Nominations for the Cheshire County Council as affecting Runcorn, close, this (Friday) afternoon. The Deputy Returning Officer is Mr George Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn Union). Notices of the vacancies in Run. corn NAth and South respectively have been issued,and the pot log day fixed for the 25th March. . . .</p>
        <p>There i—s not likely to be a contest in either North or South Divieione of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The two retiring councillors are Messrs A R Norman Liberal (North), and Mr E Imison, C. (South). Mr Nor- man, it will be remembered, succseded the late Ur lip Speakman and has only been in office a very short period. With regard to Mr Imison, however, he has served the full term, and occupies a very important office as a member of the Finance Committee of the Council. Ms services to Runcorn have been eminently useful, especi.lly his ability displayed in the direction of the advancement of technical education in the dis- trict. We feel the distrtc7. could not do better than pre.. seive the balance, and retain the services of two such very eminent townsmen. . _</p>
        <p>Tile retiring representative for Frodsharn (Mr T Baxter, of Dutton Bali), appurs to be tbe only candi- date in the field, and it is very likely that be too will be returned unopposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON. Pancake Feast at The!wall.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Pancake Feast at The!wall.</p>
        <p>Thanks to th,3 generosity of Mr R A Naylor, the villagers of Thelwall were on Shrove Tuesday regaled with pancakes in the Temperance Hall. A large number partook of the spread, and during the evening, the Sanknv 13-I.le. s %Ile Voice Choir, under the con- ctorship _et ics T Brough, gave several choruses its a er..itaele manner. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to them for their ser- vices, Their contributions included "Here's health and life to England's King" which elicited un- stinted applause. A Local Constable's Sum:len Death.</p>
        <p>Police Constable Corcoran, of the Warrington Police Force, died very suddenly at his residence its Knutsford-road, Latchford, shortly after fire o'clock on Thursday morning. It appears that at noon on Wednesday he was about to go on duty and was reaching for his overcoat,when he collapsed and fell unconscious on the floor. Dr Guest Gornall was called in and found the deceased was suffering from an epileptic stroke on the right side, Subsequently Dr Smith attended him. Dececeased gradually be- came worse and died at 5-10 on the following morniug, never regaining consciousness. Constable Corcoram who was 53 years of age had been a member of the police force since July 1880:. Previous to joining the force he served seven years in the Royal Irish Const4bulary. He lost his wife three years ago, and recently his 83D, who has been on active service with the Dragoon Guards, returned home with his right arm severely wounded. The deceased had not been in robust health for some time past. The funeral will take place at the War- rington Cemetery at 230 this afternoon. . Concert in the Masonic Hall. .</p>
        <p>A large audience assembled in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on Monday evening, when, under the auspices of the Stockton Heath Choral Society, a sacvssf al concert was held. An attractive pro- gramme was submitted, the following artistes, in addition to the members of the Society,taking part; —Mrs W B Roberts, solo pianoforte ; Miss Nellie Phoenix (Manchester) contralto; Mr Sherlock, Mr II Bennett and Mr A 0 Clay, elocutioni4; the Or- pheus and String Quartette. The entertainmentcom menced with a chorus "Down in the flow'ry vale" which received capital treatment at the hands ofthe Choral Society. Other selections were given during the evening with equal success. Mr H Sherlock gained the hearty plaudits of the audience by his expressive interpretation of "The Vagabonds,* whilst Mr Li Bennett was beard to advantage in "Sweet and Low," for whic,) he was heartily en- cored. The string quartette (Messrs Poster, John- son Burrows and Balmfor I) contributed two selec- tions in capital style, and Mrs W B Roberts effici- ently rendered two solos on the pianoforte. Miss Phoenix effectively sang "The psalm of life and a 'cello solo by Mr Balm- ford was well received. Mr A 0 Clay's recita- tion of " Reubenstein at the piano" was a pleasing item, and so deligoted the audience that the artiste was vociferously recalled. The Orpheus Quartette earned high encomiums for their contri- butions, being encored for their rendition of "Simple Simon." Praise was due to Mr J M Muir, A R C 0, who conducted the chorus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
