      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>Mmenve —B.fore Mr F Monks (in the chair), Alderman Heaton, Counoillor Smethurst, Masers Wright and Thornton. A DiteTITIITE FAMILY:—Joseph Lowe, a labourer, was ehergsd with neglecting to maintain his wife and seven children, whereby they became chargeable to the common fund of the Warrington Union.—Mr. Anklend, relieving officer, said he visited the prisoner's home on Saturday. There was neither fire nor food In the house, and the children were very poorly clothed. He provided the family with some food and made inquiries at Mr Peacock's coal yard. where the prisoner was employed. He'sacertained that th 3 man could earn as much As 5s and 61 per day by loading anal ; and at the least Li per Week. He was in regular employment as Messrs Richard- son's until a short time before Christmas. and was In receipt of 21s per week but he lost the situation through his own misconduat.—Mr. B. Shearer said he was informed that the prisoner received his wages daily and spent them before going benee.loHls wife had received nothing from him since the previous Tuesday, when he gave her 2s. The family was in very riestitute condition.—The Beneh committed the prisoner to gaol for the maximum term, three months with bard labour. MICE A BlnLlt DatTEIS FIRE BNOIHE "—THIS CHM CoisSTABL.,'S TIXPERINCE On A °Bus.—JOhn Irewey, 69. Orford-street, was &amp;weed with being drunk whilst in charge -of a pair of horses and a 'bus in Buttermarket-street at eight o'clock on Satnrday night—The Chief Constable informed the magistrates that he travelled by the bus driven by the defendant,whieh left the Cemetery Hotel at 7.40. The vehicle was driven from One side Of the road to the other In a reck- less fashion, at a speed at which he did not think a Warrington 'bus horse Was Capable of travelling. It was melee like a badly driven fire engine than a peacenble omnibus. On reaChing the Pollee Station he (Mr Talbot) sent P.C. Leonard to the de- fendant. The officer found him to be in a very drunken condition, and it appeared that be was refused drink at the Ring's Arms Hotel an early as Seven o'clock. because he was at that time considered by the landlady to be heirless. This sort of thing was becoming a crying evil on Saturday nights. He was snrp-iserl to see the defendant In that position. because he was well known to bee careful, steady driver—Defendant was fined Sc end costs. FoOTBSLLING IN THE STERET.—drOur boys named dohn Hargreaves, Thomas Braithwaite. William Rudd, and George Monks, were stinamoned for playing football in Stevenson- stmet on the Nth inst.—P C. Clough proved the case and the defendants were each fined ss.</p>
        <p>CA.B Daivana FlNED.—Patrtak Fitzpatrick, a cab driver, "WU fined 51 for leaving a horse and carriage standing in 'Horsemarket-street for an hour and a quarter without any. one to take charge of it on the 9th Inst.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>illoicamt.—Bafore Masers. W. Timmins, and R. /misass,</p>
        <p>LICENS/NG.—Ternporary authority was granted Sarah Ann Crmson. from Harry llrmson, of the " Manchester House.' Bridge-street,and to Joseph Niokson. from Jonathan Hughes. the previous holder of a Refreshment and Billiard Liocnce, in Bleb-street Runcorn. OTHeR 01IFF.NCV8 —Joseph Morris. Alfred Kennedy. and John Basnett, Runcorn youths. were summoned for causing bis Majesty's subjects. annoyance in Cooper-street, on the 3r4 of February.- The case against Kennedy was dismissed, but the other two were fined 4s 6,1 and bound over in 40s to keep the peace for six months.—W Hancock. Wellington- street. was fined 10s for using obscene language —Michael Gallagher. Sutton. was fined 5s for being drunk on February I.oth.—Rrnest Williamson. for fnrionsly driving a horse and cart in Shaw-street, on the 11th of February, whereby much danger was occasioned to the pubile, was fined 20s and Si Sri costs.—Abraham Kennedy, Joseph Moores. John Acton, and Joseph Linfard, all Runcorn young men, were fined 6s and 95 64 costs, for being drunk and disorderly in Church-street, on the 10th FAsruary.—Arthur Moores. summoned by his mother for stealing a tea check "valued 5d and which he exchanged for a mouth organ, was ordered to receive 12 strokes of the birch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>AFTER-EFFECTS OF INFLUENZA,</head>
        <p>THE AFTER-EFFECTS OF INFLUENZA,</p>
        <p>A Young Man suffering from the after-effects of Influenza got so low that the doctor had given up hopes of his recovery.</p>
        <p>Dodd's Kidney Pills quickly restored him to health.</p>
        <p>Influenza itself is not directly the cause of excAssive Mott silty, yet its 'fits produce such a lowerinf; of the forces of the system, that it leaves the natient ready to fall an easy victim .to many other maladies._ . -</p>
        <p>This explains why m many people, saffering from various form of kidney disease, des the commence- ment of thcir troulo's from the time they had an attack of influenza, and such ailments as backache, dropsy. heart troubles, pleurisy, extreme weakness, nervous de- bility, etc., can be directly traced to th 3 after-cffects of Influenza.</p>
        <p>That Dodd's Kidney Pills are invaluable to those Suffering from disease sod weakness resulting from the effects of Influenza, is proved by the many letters re- ceived from those who were suffering from the various complaints mentioned above. The following exract is from a letter from Mrs Stockhill, who resides at 3, Hope-terrace, Norton, Melton, Yorks who after men- tioning the great benefit she herself had received from Dodd's Kidney Pills, says regarding her son's case : " I should also like to let you know that I have a son who had a very sewre attack of Influenza, which left him ver - weak, and afterwards developed into Pleurisy, and in not, the doctor gave tap hopes of his recovery. After giving him all kinds of medicine, I remembered the good Dodd's Kidney Pills ha l done me, and! gave him a dose, and almost immediately one could see the reamic effect, and in a few weeks he was well and back to his work, thanks to Dcdd's Kidney Pills, which I im .sure saved his life." mpg ) AMU&amp; STOCHRILL Dodd's Kidney Pills are sold by all Chemists, 2. 9d. Der box, six boxes for 134. 91. ; or sent post free on receipt of price by The Dodd's Medicine Co., 23, Fatting- don Avenue, London, Remember D-o-d-d-'s is the original</p>
