      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>Burglary and Murder by a Cat—lnteresting Case. At the Warrington County:Court on Thuraday,last week his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands gave judg- ment in the case brought by Bertram James Brad- barY,77, Laira-street against George Hill, 83, Laira- Street, in which he claimed .£1 10s, the value of three pigeons killed by the defendant's cat. When the case was previously before the Court, it was stated on behalf of the plaintiff that on the night of the lfit'i November, the plaintiff securely locked up his pigesns in a cote. On the following morn- ing he found a window of the cote broken, three pigeons killed,and the defendant's tom-cat still in the cote. It was contended that the cat bad broken the window by jumping against it and breaking the glass in order to get into the cote. For the defence Mr, Browne, argued that such an event was rather too extra- ordinary to believe. The cat in question was eleven years old and the defendant had never known it to seize pigeons before. —His Honour in giving judg- ment on Thursday said that although the matter was very small in amount and although the inter- est involved in one sense was very insignificant, the deciding of it had involved a very great deal of inquiry on his part to see whether there was any authority or any authorities that were at all conclusive on the :point, His Honour proceeded to quote the cases he had read bearing on the subject, and said he had been, unable to find that there was any liability thrown ' on the owners ot animals of this kind unless under statute and apart from scienter. In this case it seemed to him that the defendant was not liable. It had been pressed on him that the cat broke the window of the cote, and in the absence of evidence to the contrary, he must come to the conclusion that this cat of eleven years of age did on this occasion so far forget the proprieties that were to be looked for from a respectable member of the feline tribe that it broke the window in question and destroyed three of the plaintiffs pigeons. But in order to sustain the action he held that it must be shown that the defendant knew that the cat had committed or was likely to eommit such an act. There was nothing in the evidence to prove that this cat was of vicious habits. It was true that a cat might be expected to kill birds, but no cat with respect for itself, especially a cat of quiet habits and one which had attained a venerable age like the one in question, could be expected to break a window in this ferocious manner. He had come to the conclusion to find for the defendant, but if Mr Longton desired to appeal every assist- ance would be given him.—Mr Longt on said there would be no appeal.—His Honour said his judg- ment was for the defendant without costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BILE BEANS IN LANCASHIRE. A Sprint Runner Cured.</head>
        <p>BILE BEANS IN LANCASHIRE. A Sprint Runner Cured.</p>
        <p>Of Pains in the Back, Dizziness, and Liver</p>
        <p>Disorder. Mr John Malkin Myatt, a miner, of Oxford-street, Castle Hill, Hindley, near Wigan, was formerly well known in Lancashire as a sprint runner. Unfortunately he was stricken down by illness some years ago Charles Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness were intro- duced, and have now restored him to perfect health so that when a " Wigan Examiner" reporter who had beard of his case called upon hiwi, in gratitude for his cure, Mr Myatt gave the following particulars for publi- cation :—" Every morning when I awakened" said he, "my head achsd so badly that it might have been actua ly splitting open. I felt sick and altogether wretched. Knowing the danger of going to work on an empty stomach, I tried my best before leaving the house to drink a small cup of tea, and eat a litttle solid food. This however would never stop down, and as I walked to work I used to suffer terribly with a nasty hatching up' of wind and liquid. I had a nasty pain in the back too, which was sometimes so bad that I could scarcely walk for it. _ For nearly three years I suffered in that</p>
        <p>way,and at the end of that period I was in a sorry plight. I was losing flesh at a rapid rate, and no matter what I ate or drank it did not seem to do me any good what- ever. I had always a dreadfully dull and tired feeling, and but for a merciful Providence putting Charles Forde's Bile Beans in my way, I sincerely believe that I should have had to give up work altogether. One day while reading the paper I came across an advertisement, in which I noticed a ease similar to my own which had been cured by Charles Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness. Strange to relate I bad scarcely put down the paper before there was a knock at the door, and a man left a sample of those very beans. I took the sample Beans in proper doses, and they did me a little good. Then I bought a box and began a thorough course. The first effect I experienced was an attack of what I thought was diarrhcea ; but I soon began to feel that the Beans were what they were represented to be. I started to im- prove from the very day I commenced taking them. By degrees my headache ceased to trouble me, the feeling of nausea in the morning and the vomiting also dis- appeared. My breathing which had been laboured and heavy became more regular, the backache disappeared altogether, and I now take all kinds of food without being afraid of either biliousness or indigestion. What is more lam quickly patting on weight. At the present time I feel well and fit as ever I did in the whole course of my career. This I attribute solely to Charles Forde's Bile Beans." Mrs. Myatt, who bad all the time been an eager listener to the interview, here interposed and bore out her husband's statement. "Can you conscientiously recommend Bile Beans to all who suffer as you did " ? inquired the reporter.— " I most certainly can " was Mr. Myatt's reply " and have done so on every possible occasion I have had the opportunity. I have no objection whatever to this inter- view being published. In the interests of humanity, I think Bile Beans ought to be in every household, not only in the United Kingdom but in every country." Charles Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousnesir which have done so much for, and are so extolled by Mr. Myatt, by bringing about hundreds of such cures as the above, have made for themselves the reputation of being superior to all medicines for pains in the chest and back, dizziness, buzzing in the head, sickness in the morning, indigestion, liver and kidney disorder, constipation, headache, debility, nervousness, female ailments, colds, chill, rheumatism, and as a preventive of influenza. All chemists stock them, or you may obtain them direct from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Company, 119 and 120, London Wall, London, E.C.. by sending prices Is. Id. er 2s. 91. per box. (2s. 91. box contains three times Is. 11a.)</p>
