      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Defeat Broughton Rangers.</p>
        <p>The return match between Warrington and Broughton Rangers was played at Wilderspool on Saturday before 5.000 spectators- A sudden thawehad at the last moment almost, made play poasible, the ground being in very fair condition and the weather all that could be desired. At Broughton the frost had not relaxed its iron grip on the slit, and in consequence the Rangers expected that the match would have Co be abandoned at Warrington. The telegram informing them of the ground's fitness bus caused some inconvenience and they arrived at Warrington a man short. W James and Hogg were also absent in the back division, their places being taken by Barrett and Hampson. Warrington turned out as selected, the team under the control of Mr B Ashton. Oldham, being as follows: (Warrington) Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Hockenhall, and Harris, three-quarter backs; Bate and Burgess, half-backs; Morrison, Eden, Scholtze, Swift, Boardman, Fell, Cunningham and Ed- moedson, forwards. Broughton Rangers:— Harry, back; Widdeson, Chapman, Wilson, and Sampson, three- quarter back; Barrett and S James, half-backs; White- head, Ruddick, Thompson, Scott, Cleminson, Robinson and Hardisty, forwards. Warrington lost the toss and Swift set the ball in motion against both wind and sun. There was no reply and Warrington got a footing in the visitors' half. 9 James secured and passing out well be gave his three- quarters an opportunity which they utilised so well that Widdeson was only grassed at the Warrington quarter. Warrington retaliated with short passing runs and reached the centre again, Isherwood leading the attack With a brilliant run through the Rangers' ranks. A slow piece of fielding by Hallam lost ground, but Fish was to the fore with a run and punt into the opposite half. Bate and Burgess smartly got away, Hockenhall and Harris carrying OR the movement before Widdeson accounted for the wing man. A quick rush to the line broke down the Broughton defence, Fish throwing to Bate who scrambled over at the corner only to be brought back for an obviously forward pass. War- rington held on to their advantage in position and 9 James was twice penalised for being too eager. The second time was in front of the posts and Fish kicked a goal against the wind Warrington played up powerf ally getting the ball ant well and making progress with quick passing. Harris cleared all but the full back epee, Harry however bringing him down in good style. Chap- man and Widdeson responded with a clever effort which ended in touch, owing Co the resolute defence of Harris and Hockenhall. Warrington drove the Rangers back again, Isherwood playing a splendid game in the centre, but comieg in for some severe handling, Fish and he changed places for a time, and the former broke away beautifully, passing to Isherwood at the right moment. The latter looked like scoring when the treacherous turf caused his downfall. Half-time came with the score reading : Warrington, one goal (2 points) ; Broughton Rangers, nil. Thompson re-started,Morrison and Wilson exehanging long kicks, the latter sending to touch. Warrington got down by passing between Burgess, Hockenhall and Fish. Hallam punted well into the goal-mouth, and the home forwards brushing aside opposition Morrison secured, and was over the line when be threw wide, Widdesen intercepting and eff mting a clearance. The Rangers showed their appreciation of the let-off by taking the offensive. Chapman got away cleverly and transferring to Widdeson, the latter ran clear only to be brought back by the touch-judge when he did not appear to have been in touch. Fine combination between S James, Chapman and Wilson kept Warrington at bay, the last named running very strongry on one occasion. Fish changed the venue with a couple of huge punts at a time when Burgess wail off the field, with a damaged knee. He was able to return in about ten minutes when Warrington again attacked, Fish tackling Harry near the line. Scrimmages followed and the home backs securing, passed rapidly across to Fish who was knocked in touch. Broughton made several good attempts to get *veep...hue Warrington held their own and won by a goal to nil. Warrington v. Oldham.</p>
        <p>The postponed fixture between Warrington and Old- ham was played on Shrove Tuesday, at,' Oldham,in the presence of 5,000 spectators. Good weather prevailed, but the ground was in a terribly heavy state owing to the frost and thaw. Warrington had rather a weak team. Hallam (full back), Burgess (half back) and Edmondson (forward), being absentees. Oldham were short of Huzzey, S Leas and Fereusson, capable substi- tutes being found, tesWeVer. Mr Newtoo,of Altrincham, was the referee, sad the, team were composed as follows : Warrington : Ratcliffe, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Hockenhall and Harris, threequarters ; J T Bate and It Bate, half backs ; Boardman, Morrison, Eden, Fell, Scholtze,Cunningharn, Pemberton, and Swift, forwards. Oldham : Thomas, full back ; Fletcher. Civil, T Davies and S Williams, three quarters ; A Lees and Lawton, half backs; Moffatt, Telfer, Wilkinson, Vowles, Frater, Broome, Wormald and Ellis forwards. Swift started for Warrington, who early on became aggressive, scrimmages being fought out near the home line. Harris nearly dribbled over, only Thomas's smart- ness saving the Oldham side from disaster. Fletcher removed play to the centre, where Hockenhall got hold and was making his way back again when Davies inter- cepted and kicked across to Williams who was pushed in touch near the corner flag. Keen play followed in the Warrington half, Davies failing dismally with a drop. Oldham secured possession again and Civil re- ceiving at centre dodged the Warrington backs and ran clean through, Ratcliffe making a poor attempt to stop him. Tha try behind the posts was easily improved upon by Wormald. Oldham played on the Warrington full-back and nearly scored again, Elockenball coming to the rescue when the home for- wards had reached the line. 9 Williams was the next to get away, but HockPnhall let him down. Then War- rington had a turn, J T Bate dodging from the scrimm- age and neglectine Fish when the latter had only Fletcher to pass. Eden took a pass mean:. for Davies and was gaing strongly for the line when the whistle sounded—hard lines. The home team returned to the attack and Williams scored from a pass by A Lees, Worrnald failing at goal. Warrington worked their way back again to the centre, Thomas losing ground by kicking in touch. Nothing further happened before the interval when the sctre was :—Oldham 8 points, War- rington nil. Moffat resumed play and R Bate was tackled. Old- ham at once passed out Fletcher and Civil running down to Ratcliffe who smartly accounted for the latter. Play proceeded at the Warrington quarter, and Fletcher getting hold eluded several opponents and scored " on his own," no goal resulting. Warrington next bad a brief spell at attacking, Bate and Fish coming very near to scoring with neat combination. Davies once more transferred play to the Warrington end where there was some keen play. Warrington looked like keeping the line intact, but just on time Fletcher scored again at the corner from a wide pass. Full score :—Old- ham, 1 goal, 4 tries, (14 points) ; Warrington nil. Swinton v Stockport. At Stockport Oram kicked oft facing a strong wind, and for a time Stockport's tactics, in keeping the ball close, succeeded, but eventually Davies and Morgan passed out to R Valentine, who gave the ball to Hemp- eon, and that player gained a try for Swinton. R Valentine placed a goal. This was quickly followed by H Valentine and Morgan. in turn, scoring unimproved tries for the visitors. Then Stockport had a turn, and after Worsley had lost the ball over the goal line Booth scored an unimproved try for Stockport. The second portion of the game was much in favour of the S win- tonians, but Stockport defended their line in such a vigorous manner that only once did the visitors break through. when It Valentine added a try and placed a coal. Final score :—Swinton, two goals and four tries (16 points), Stockport one try (three points). Widnes v. Rochdale Hornets. At Widnes, in beautiful weather, bet on a muddy I ground. Neither team was fully represented. Widnee played with a strong wind in the first half, and after a smart run by Peacock, Rispan kicked across the field to Hughes, who ran in easily, Baby failing at goal. After- wards play was fairly even, bothsends being repeatedly visitor'. Towards the interval Lally got over for Widnes, but offside spoiled the effort. Douglas scored, Half- time score :—Widnes, two tries ; Hornets, none. Aspey re-etarted for Widnes, Exciting play ensued, the home forwards playing smartly together. Wightman again received possession, and was almost over the line, Aspel, the effecting a good save. The Hornets nnw kept up</p>
