      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES COOPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES CO- OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual• Tea Party and Concert.</p>
        <p>On Monday night the annual tea party was held in connection with the above society at Lacy-street School- room. After the tea a concert was given under the presidency of Mr Willau. There was a crowded audience and the programme from first to last was a thoroughly enjoyable one. "The Moorish Maid" and "Esmeralda!' were sung by Miss Minnie Keating with capital effect the artiste,who possesea a beautiful soprano volce,infue- ing a world of pathos into her selections. Miss Evelyn Archer, a contralto singer, whose range of voice was considerable, rendered "Daddy" and "The Carnival" with an expression surpassing words, and was deser- vedly recalled. Mr Sydney Dawson's deep bass voice was heard to advantage in the fine songs entitled "Marching" and "Out on the Deep," "Alice! where art thou?' and "An evening song" were capitally sung by Mr Roberts Kay. The neJat pleasing moments may be ascribed to the part songs,which were entitled "A dream of paradise," the battle eve,"A regular Royal Queen." - "Life's Dream is o'er "and "Sleep, gentle sleep," which were rendered by the artistes already mentioned, some of the items being given in a striking and beauti- ful manner. The humorous selections were given by Mr F Maitland, whose efforts to please were appreciated by everyone, each of his pieces being devoid of vulgarity while brimful of bumcur. During the interval an address was delivered by Mr Thorpe, director of the Co- operative Wholesale Society, who congratulated the society on their increasing membership, and their large trade. Proceeding to the balance sheet, he criticised somewhat severely the smallness of the amount—a sum of £s—which bad been devoted to educational purposes when the profits for the half-year totalled £ll,OOO. He pointed out in detail the vast amount of good which had been done in Ireland and the agricultural distriets for the betterment of the working classes by adopting co- operation. He advocated that the educational side must be cultivated and that the social aspect must not be forgotten in the eernmercial ietereste which were necessarily involved. Mr A E Spafford acted as pianist with RO little ability. The Chairman, it may be men- tioned, wa supported by —masers Cozier, Dyer, Priestly, a Bcragg, Stubbs, Hartles and Rawlineon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>--40-..-- SORE THROAT.</head>
        <p>--40-..-- SORE THROAT.</p>
        <p>If you suffer from a sore and congested throat, if you your voice is hard, and if pyaoinhave to cough in order to every ry swallow now an watbaoauint n' if Clear the passage from the lungs, it is quite evident you I are in a very serious state of ill-heath, and should exer- cise the greatest care, and, if possible, stay indoors and in one warm atmosphere until you are better again. I The best remedy in all the world for sore throat and coughs and chills is care, combined with systematic doses of that far-famed and ever successful remedy for chest and throat affections—Lavan's Lung Linctus. People in Lancashire swear by this last, and always keep it handy. Try a bottle yourself, and juCge by its effect- You can get Ldvr co-p., and aLnid stneptuosalretaenTsagfoeolyd chemist's, drug stores, o packed in bottles, is lit, 2s. 9d. and 4s. 61, from the proprietors, Green and Co., Chemists, Bolton.</p>
        <p>Catholic Dance at the Parr Hall,, There was a happy gathering in Parr Hall on Monday evening on the occasion of the annual dance in connection with St Mary's Roman Catholic Church, Latchford. No fewer than 350 young people were present and a programme of 22 dances was gone through. Excellent arrangements had been made by a committee and the hall had been scented for the occasion by Messrs Joseph Crosfleld and Sons. Owing to the national bereavement, the Mayor was unable to be present. Messrs P Delaney and U ri Wells discharged the duties of M.C.'s and they were assisted by the following stewards: Messrs G Dolan, J W Bibby, P Bate and T Curran. Dancing commenced at 8 p.m. to the strains of Mr Percy Randall's excellent band, and was continued with zest until 2.30 the following morning. •</p>
