        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. TEE pessimist's only consolation is the hope that he will be able to say, "I told you so!" Mosr of us would be all right if our actions were not so much in arrears of our intentions.</p>
        <p>THE British Lion and American Eagle seem per- fectly %%Ming to lie down together, but neither can make up his mind to lie down first. COMPARED with the amount a man wants in this :world, it is surprising how little he can manage to get along with. TliE Drat baby gets its photograph taken every three months; the other babies are lucky to get theirs taken once in three years. " WHAT is all that racket next door ? " "Well —it's toe Bobbses; they are either having a fight or holding a peaee jubilee." EACHER : "What is a ' vacuum ?" Bright Boy : •• My pop says it's somethin' that most of the soldier boys has got inside 'ern." ROCHELLE : " How much did that eyes oast you, old man?" Parka: "I don't know; my deeter hasn't sent In his bill yet. FARMER "Doctor, my nerve is gone." Dentist: "Oh, no, it 'ait till I get a firm hold, and you'll find it." IT is surprising how many good things a man reads in the course of an evening that should teach a lesson to his neighbours. AbIBIGUOUS.—" Johnson wants to borrow some money of me. "Do you know anything about him?" "I know him as well as Ido you. I wouldn't let him have a farthing." ,SPOKE FEELINGLY.—Younger Sister (sentiment- " They say that love Is blind." Elder ister "And dumb, too, I think." Tn; USUAL THlNG.—After all his sufferings, the poor artist died, and his soul was wafted to heaven. " Sky!cl again I" he exclaimed. _</p>
        <p>Ni; NEED To asx.—Mother : "You did not ask twice tor cake when you took tea with Mrs. Prim, did you?" Glayds : No, mamma—" Mother: "That was right." Gladys "I helped myself." MORE MODEST.—" Does the professor really expect to discover the North Pole?" "No; he mere.ly expects to be known as the man who failed to di-cover it in 1898."</p>
        <p>M EETIN G THE CASE. —Secretary : "This play doesn't seem to take." Manager: "Make tre- mendous success' in the advertisements twics as large as it is now." CUSTOMER (to baker's boy) : IS your bread nice and light! Baker's boy (confidentially) : Yes ma'am; it only weighs ten ounces to the pound. Mr. STOUTER "An heirloom, Johnnie, is gurnething that's handed down from father to son." Little Johnnie: " Huh I That's a funny name !or Fin tS. " "THAT man Fraser doesn't borrow my lawn- mower so often nowadays." " How did you cure him of the habit?" "Every time he asked for it I sent and borrowed his bicycle." Dowri "Papa, do they get salt out of Salt Lake?" Papa: "Yes, dear, large quantities." Dolly: "And ink out of the Black Sea ? " Papa: "No; now keep quiet." Dolly: "Are there any women on the Isle of Man?" THERE ARE OTHERS. —Grimshavr : " Pensmith, who writes so much magazine poetry, doesn't look like a poet, does he ? " Teller: No." Grim- shaw : Well, he isn't." "ARE the natives of the island Christianised?" asked the sombre lady. "They would be," sighed the escaped missionary, "if their diet had any effect." ILLUMINATED. —Rev. Dr. Thumper: "Does not married life seem brighter to you?" Mrs. New. : "You can imagine. My bridal gifts in- cluded twenty-five lamp's." WHAT IT WAS.—" Just listen how they are pounding the floor with their feet !" said the tragedian behind the curtain. "Yes," said the leading ingenue; "that is the stamp of public approval." HIS FEELINGS. —Friend : "Is that a bright baby where you live ? " The Skye Terrier: "No ; it's a stupid little thing. It sometimes Lakes me for a cat and calls me 'Pussy A WORD OF COMMENDATION.—" There was one moment," wrote the dramatic critic of the Cyclone County Sandbag, "when Mr. Hamlet, who played Othello, was simple, natural, and unaffected. That was when the egg struck him." COUNTERBALANCED. —Sally Gay: "What do you think of Miss Bramley ? " "I like her very well; for, while she is so exasperatingly clever, she is also consolingly ugly." WITH A RESERVATION.—The Proud Father: "Oh ! come, now! You were a boy yourself once." The Irate Neighbour: "Maybe I was; but I didn't have an idiot of a father to encourage me to make myself an infernal nuisance!" A SURE SIGN. —Visitor : "So your poor husband has passed away, Mrs. Murphy. He died happy, I hope?" Mrs, Murphy: Oi think so, mum. The last thing he did was to crack me over the head wid a medicine bottle!"</p>
        <p>WANTED A CHANGE.—" Ido wish the weather would change," exclaimed the dyspeptic man's wife. "But it isn't nearly so disagreeable as it night be." "I know that., But it makes My husband so unhappy not to have more to complain , , about." INQursinvE TOURIST: "And how do you find the crops this year, Murphy?" Murphy: "How do I find the crops, is it? Sure, your honour, 'tie by digging for 'em, any way!" ENTIRELY SUCCESSFUL. Doctor: "Did your visit to the seaside have the desired effect, madam?" Mrs. Fullpurse : "Oh, yes, doctor; both my daughters are married." SILENT.—" How do you pronounce the last syllable of that word butterine,'" asked the cus- tomer. "The last syllable is silent" stiffly replied the grocer. HIT IT THE FIRST TIME. —MTH. Matchman "Look how earnestly Rose and Mr. Beach are bidding each other good•night at the gate. lam sure there's something between them." Mr. Matchman : "So am I; it's the gate." TAUGHT BY EXPERIENCE. —Husband : "What would you say, my dear, if I were to offer you the money for a new bonnet?" Wife: "I wouldn't say a word, darling, until I had the money in my hands, for fear you'd change your mind." Nor APPREHENSIVE. First Boarder: "The landlady has gone to a food exhibition, but I don't think it can possibly affect us." Second Boarder: " Why should it affect us?" First Boarder: "Well, of course, if she finds any cheaper kinds of food than we are getting now, we'll have to eat them; but I don't think it's possible." APPRECIATED IT.—" Ah, there's nothing like the bicycle. I've been unable to sleep for years, and—" "You rode a machine and it cured you?" "No; but a cyclist ran into me and I was insensible for several minutes." GREAT FininEs.—" His success in a financial way has been something marvellous." " Yes ?" "Yes, sir. I've often heard him tell] how, when he came here fifteen years ago, all he owed in the world was a sovereign or two, and last week he failed for ten thousand." _</p>
        <p>"DUMB" Atinua.s.—A small boy was pelting stones at, a barking dog when a passer-by stopped and addressed him. "Little boy, don't you know you should be kind to dumb animals?" " as, sir, I am ; but he isn't dumb." NOT WHAT HE WANTED.—(Scene : Kentish Farm). Tramp: "Could you give a poor man a little assistance or find him some employment?" —Farmer: "You can have some work on the spot. I have a heap of wood yonder."—Tramp: "Hum —ha, yes—l'll send you the man directly. It's not for myself I sin seeking a job, but for a friend oi mine."</p>
        <p>How IT TURNED OUT USEFUL.—" Never VMS glad for this itn-im•pediment in my speech but once," said the man from the North. "When was that'?" "Fe-fe-fellow asked me h-h-how much I Would take for a-n horse, and while LI-I was t-trying to tell him t-tweuty p-pounds, he offered me f-fifty" BRINGING IT 1102,1 E.—" Woman," he hissed, " womr-u, do you thus spurn my heart after lead- ing me on ? " "When did I lead you on, as you call it?" asked the girl. "Did you not tell me that that fortune-teller had told you that you were to wed a handsome, blonde young man, with the grace of a Greek god and the voice of an &amp;a- lien harp ? " LOSING No TIME—A Paris shopkeeper wrot to one of his customers as follows :—" I am able to offer you cloth like the enclosed sample at nine francs the metre. In case Ido not hear from you, I shall conclude that you wish to pay only eight francs. In order to lose no time, I accept the last- mentioned price."</p>
