      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MISS GOULD, (From a photograpb.)</head>
        <p>MISS GOULD, (From a photograpb.)</p>
        <p>We have two letters about the health of a young lady to which we call special attention, The first one tells how an illness left her in a state of decline, which grew worse until her parents feared the dreaded consumption was taking hold of her system and dragging her down. The first letter is u follows :</p>
        <p>" Nr. White Horse, West Street, Bedminster, Bristol, '• May Bth, 1900. " Gentlemen,—l should be lacking in gratitude did I not testify to the remarkable efficacy of Scott's Emulsion, so far as I have preyed it in connection with the recovery of my daughter from an illness out of which little hope was entertained that she would ever emerge. Last July she was taken with a complaint which gradually grew into what we thought was consumption. She was then seventeen years of age, an age which, it will be readily ad- mitted, wab a very critical one. Her appetite en- tirely deserted her and only very dainty delicacies would tempt her. Medicine seemed to have no effect upon her, and her vitality diminished until she was incapable of the least exertion ; in fact, she was nothing but skin and bone, and the cause of the greatest anxiety to us. her parents. Ultimately a medical gentleman in our parish, whom we called in to see her, recom- mended Scott's Emulsion. So, last February, we began giving her Scott's Emulsion, expecting, as with the other medicines, it would do her no good. In this, however, we were agreeably deceived, for she showed signs of improvement in a remarkably short space of time. We followed up the Etnuliion till the present tinoe,and my daughter is fast putting on flesh, while her appetite enables her to eat heartily almost anything. She is continuing this excellent remedy, and we are hopeful that she will soon be as healthy and strong as ever. I am sure that Scott's Emulsion worked this agreeable wonder, and if by my testimony to its worth it may cause others to give it a trial, the beneficial rebults of which I em quite sanguine, I shall have conferred at least some benefit on suffering humanity.--Grate- fully yours, '(Signed) ALFRED Gour.n." The second letter was received six months later, and speaks for itself :</p>
        <p>2, Edith Place, West Street, Bedminster, Bristol, November sth, 1900, "Dear Sirs,—This letter is to supplement my let...</p>
        <p>, .. . ter of testimony to you of last May, and to say that my daughter has been continuing the use of Scott's Emulsion up to the last few weeks, and I am very happy to be able to say she has been completely restored to good health. I am indeed thankful that we tried Scott's Emulsion,for it effected a cure after everything else tried had failed to do any good.— With my hearty good wishes for the success of Scott's Emulsion, Believe me, Yours very truly, (Signed), ALFRED GOULD." Such is the story briefly told, of only one of the thousands of similar eases that are happening every day in about every part of the British Empire. There is no exaggeration of any detail. In tact Mr Gould could not begin to reveal in such short Space the real anxiety over the health of his daughter,and the point to especially emphasise perhaps is his real motive for taking the trouble to write at all. After trying many remedies he finally found one that is really effectual in overcoming the wasting and ema- ciated tendencies of the system, and, as he says, be is glad to make his experience of service to others. If these two letters lead you to restore health to a child or adult through Seott's Emulsion, will yeu not be glad you took the trouble to read them ? Scott's Emulsion is not a secret nostrum made from worth.. lees or harmful drug.. It is simply cod-liver oil, hypophosphites and glycerine blended into a form that is palatable, and which anybody can take and digest. It saves the stomach and intestines the work of digestion, and at the same time is readily absorbed into the blood. Wherever there is a tendency to consumption or chronic coughs, colds, weak lungs or emaciation. Scott's Emulsion will be found the most effectual and most satisfactory remedy that can be employed. Children that do not thrive derive the greatest possible benefit from its use. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by 1 sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bowne, Limited, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London. E.C., and mentioning this paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
