      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR H P MORTIMER.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR H P MORTIMER.</p>
        <p>At FrAsharn Parish Church on Thursday week noon, the marriage was solemnized of Mr. Harry Percival Mortimer, eldest son of the late Mr. William Mortimer, of Beaconhurst, Frodsham, and ,formerly of Orford, Warrington, and Miss Muriel Ida Mary Thomas, the younger daughter of Mi. Alfred Thomas, of Hemp Gill, Frodsham. The church was crowded, great interest being taken in the event. An awning had been erected from the main entrance to the outside gate. Over the entrance were the words " Longlife and happiness." The ceremony was performed by the Rev E Charley, vicar of Ince (brother-in-law of the bride), assisted by the Rev H B Blogg, ALA., vicar of Frodsham. A full choir was in attendance, and the service was choral. Dr. Bridge, of Chester, officiated at the organ. The bride was charmingly attired in a dress of white satin trimmed with duchesse lace and orange blossoms, and wore a spray of orange blossoms and veil of tulle. She carried a beautiful white bouquet composed of lilies of the valley and other flowers; her ornaments being a diamond and pearl harp pendant and chain, gifts of the bridegroom, and a brooch of pearl and turquoise,the gift of Mr Robert Davies (uncle of the bride). She was given away by her father,her brides- maid being Miss Mortimer(sister of the bridegroom) who looked very pretty in a becoming costume of white crepe de chine Oita-mod with gold passe- menterie, and a toque of blue . yelvet trimmed with violate. She carried a lovely bouquet,' tied with blue streamers, and wore a pearl pendant and chain, gifts of the bridegroom. Mr William Mortimer (brother of the bridegroom) effectively discharged the duties ot best man. The wedding party left the church to the strains of Mendelssohn's " Wedding March," and were greeted outside with the c us- tomary showers of confetti and orange blossom,while the bells rang out merry peals and continued at intervals throughout the day. A reception was subsequently held by Mr and Mrs Alfred Thomas at Hemp Gill, lwhere a large number of guests had assembled. The happy couple left afterwards for London, where the initial portion of the honeymoon is to be spent. The bride's travelling dress was of dark blue cloth and Eton coat, with hat to match,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
