      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>e....,.../ibommtoi...l</head>
        <p>e....,.../ibommtoi...l</p>
        <p>A Queen's Guardsman. Private E Morley, of the let King's Company of Grenadier Guards, and late of the D Company War- rington Volunteers, had the honour to be one of the Queen's Guardsmen. Re was a member of the Friars' Green Young Men's Christian Association (P.S.A.) and volunteered for active service in South Africa with the first contingent of Volunteers, but was not selected. He enlisted in the Guards, and was one of the 60 picked men to go te Osborne to guafd the late Queen's coffin. They were on guard one hour at a time, and were presented with the Victorian Order medal. It is interesting to note %ha the young man's grandfather was one of the guard of honour to the Queen when she was married. He was in the Scots Greys, under Earl Poulett, Election of Overseers. At a meeting of the Finance Committee nn Mon- day, the retiring overseers, Messrs Joseph Shaw Green, Frederick William Monks, Arthur Bennett, and James Fairciough were re-elected.</p>
