      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>WRIT ISSUED AGAINST THE CORPORATION.</p>
        <p>Case to be Contested.</p>
        <p>The Town CLERK (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) received a writ on Thursday, last week, from Messrs. Hinde, Milne and Bury, solititors, Manches- ter, who are acting on behalf of Mr John ;Pemberton, of Stockton Heath, demand- ing permission to examine the manuscript minutes and accounts of the Gas Committee of the Corpora- tion, The agitation for the examination of Corpora- tion beoka,papers and contracts has been afoot for several months. In the " Examiner " of January 51h,it Was reported that at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held on the previous Monday, a communication was read, signed by 25 persona as "Members of the Ratepayers and Property Owners' Association and ot,iere," requesting that two per- sons appointed by them should be "allowed to examine all books, papers and contracts covering the last five years, with a view to• ascertaining whether any conerants or transactions had been entered into between the Corporation and any mem- ber of the Corporation or any official of the Corpora- tion, or between the Corporation and any linlited or private company in which any member of the Cor- poration or official are interested directly or indi- rectly." The TOWN CLERK advised the committee that any ratepayer was entitled to examine the orders of the Council on the Borough Treasurer for pay- ments of money, and the vouchers for such pay- ments, and was also entitled to examine the abs- tract of the Borough Treasurer's accounts after the same had been audited. It was thereupon raolved "that no obstacle has been or will be placed in the way of any ratepayer examining the accounts of the Corporation according Ito the procedure provided by the Municipal Corpora- don Acts,and that in the opinion of this committee the facilities so provided fully meet all that is reasonable in the above application, and that special facilities for the examination of the accounts of the Corporation are unnecessary." Mr. PEMBERTON'S grievance is that he has been refused permission to see the minute book of the Gas Committee. When in company with a solici- tor's clerk, he tendered the statutory fee of one shilling and asked to be furnished with the minutes of the Gas Committee for the year 1900, he was supplied with a copy of the-printed minutes as they had been submitted to the Council for confirmation A correspondence has since taken place between the Manchester firm of solicitors, already alluded to, and the Town Clerk, relative to the production of the minute book.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK pointed out that all resolutions of the committee requiring the approval of the Council were set out in the printed minutes shown to Mr Pemberton. In a subsequent letter Mr Whittle said : "The Corporation are prepared to allow any ratepayer of the borough to inspect any books which they are legally entitled to inspect in accordance with the decision in the case of Williams v. the Mayor and Corporation of Man- chester.ln my,opinion the production of the printed proceedings of the Cannel! (which 1 have before explained contain all the acts of the Gas Committee requiring the approval of the Council) is full com- pliance with the decision in the above case." The solicitors acting on behalf of Mr Pemberton have now taken the extreme action of i.suing a writ against the Corporation. We understand that the Corporation is determined to tight it, and have already entered an appearance. The case will pro- bably be heard in the Chancery Courts at London.</p>
      </div>
