      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>lttters to Mt Crittor.</p>
        <p>A Neglected Lancashire Poet.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Some time since Lancashire deservedly recog- nised the gaciui cf Benj Brierloy by erecting a statue to his memory in Queen's Park, Manchester Recently the people of Rochdale and district have erected a memorial in honour of the four writers, Edwin Waugh, Oliver Ormerod, Miss Lahee, and John Trafford Clegg. There is yet one writer whose works are known I throughout the country and whose name is a household word in almost every home in Lancashire, who is still left out in the cold. I refer to the late Samuel Laycock. Surely the Author of "Bonny Brid," "Bowton's Yard," "Ode to 'th Sun," "Cheer up a bit longer," "Th' owd Badman," and other popular poems is deserving of some public recognition. Without making any invidious comparison or in the slightest degree disparaging the works of Waugh, Brier- ley, acd the other writers I venture to predict that the dialect poems ef Laycock will live as long as any of those whose claims have been justly recognised. I can fearlessly say that no writer did more to "cheer up" the starving people of Lancashire during the trying times of the American War than did friend Laycock with his cheerful songs ot hope in those hard and dark days. Who has not yet Laycock's tender and deeply touch- ing poem commencing with the following soul-stirring stanza :</p>
        <p>Wart welcome, little bonny brid But shouldn't ha' come just when tha did, Toimes are bad; We're short o' nobbles for esur Joe, But that of course tha didn't know, Did ta lad'</p>
        <p>When this popular poem was published the "London Spectator" declared it to be worthy of the best effort of Burns. This and other of his cotton panic lyrics won for Lay- cock the well deserved title of " Laureate of the Cotton Panic." Laycock's poems, published shortly before his death, have had a large circulation and clearly show the hold he has of the hearts of the poor people of Lanca- shire. No Lancashire song writer has done mere with his purse and pen for the temperance cause than has Laycock. What have his temperance admirers done for him, nothing. To be candid they have shame fully neglected him, all through his life, nothwithstand- ing bis work and self sacrifice for their cause. They have now an opportunity of making amends for their past neglect of this popular author and I trcat they will do so and join in the promotion of some suit- able memorial to his memory. Surely the people of Blackpool, where Laycock lived for so many years, and in which town he died, will not be behind Rochdale in recognising the claims of one of her most worthy townsmen. Then there are the towns of Burnley, 01 iham, and Stalybridge, where Larcock likewise lived and wrote so many of his poems. I feel sure the people of these towns would do something in the way of paying a fitting tribute to the work and memory of Laycock.</p>
        <p>_ • No more suitable place In my opinion could be found than Blackpool in which to place a monument or stem to Layceck. Here it could be seen by all the Lancashire folk visiting this favourite health resort. As an old friend and admirer of the poet I will under- take to de in utmost in furthering the object in view. I shall be pleased to receive the names and addresses of any persons who are willing to co-operate with me in the promotion of so worthy an obPct as the suggested memorial to Laycock.—Yours faithfully, JAMES MORT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Church House, Higher Sutton, Macclesfield.</head>
