      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOROUGH</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH</p>
        <p>AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>The Forthcoming Election.</p>
        <p>Friday, March Ist, has been fixed as the day of election of two elective auditors for the County Borough of Warrington. The two retiring auditors are Messrs John Harrison and James Bolton. The former will seek re-election but Mr Bolton, who has held the office eleven years, is retiring "as a protest against the manner in which the auditors have been recently treated." A vacancy therefore occurs. Candidates must be nominated in writing, subscribed by two burgesses as proposer and seconder, and by eight other burgesses as assenting to the nomination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NOMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The nominations ihave already been received for the following candidates. Mr John Harrison, who is des- cribed as a woollen draper and tailor. living at 4, Egypt. street, has been nominated by Councillor Tinnion, seconded by Mr J Rymer Young, and assented to by the following burgesses :—Messrs Thomas B Carter, Josiah Longland, E S Garland, CE King, J B Isaac, J W Hay- ward, John Taylor, and Edward Pickavance. Mr Edwin Bradshaw,chartered accountant, Laburnum Villas, Stockton Heath (who has been nominated to supply the vacancy created by Mr Bolton's retirement), is proposed by Mr Robert Davies, seconded by Mr John White, and supported by MearsJoho HPsketh, Sidney Wallhead, John Sutton, R W R Avson. Frank Both, H M Ashton, William Gill, and Charles Holland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath 2nd v. Chester 2nd.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Stockton Heath on a very muddy ground. The visitors arrived short of three men but were provided with excellent substitutes from the Latchford club. From the bully off Stockton Heath pressed and within two minutes from the start scored their first goal, the second, third and fourth following in quiok succession the home team having almost all the game to the interval. After half time Scockton Heath still had the better of the game but owing to the deep condition of the ground and the excellent display of the visiting goal keeper, th3y could only add two goals. Five minut,ll from the finish of the game the visitors scored from a breakaway, the game thus ending in a win for the home team by six goals to one. Goals were scored for the home team, by Bond, Albioson, Willcccks, Meir, and Brierley (2) and for the visitors by Hiner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>The Rector (Rev Canon Willis) has returned to Warrington having derived considerable benefit from his vi,dt to Tialsnead Park. The rev gentle- man read the prayers at the morning and evening services at the Parish Church on Sunday, and in the afternGon delivered an address to the Sunday School scholars. DELICIOUS MAZAWATTHE TEA DELIC(OUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS MAZA VATTER TEA SoLEADEiva4s: HENRY MILLING AND Co.. LTD. cmoLESALE AIM RETAIL GROCER. 9 ETC., Ti 0111 KM 41iFCEr4T5EET. WARRING)</p>
