      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 22 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 22 1901.</p>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. Important Statement by Mr. Schalk Burger.</p>
        <p>Prolongation of Hostilities Futile</p>
        <p>PRETORTA, WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Reports have been received here from Lydenburg that Mr Schalk Burger, the acting President of the Transvaal Boers, addressing a gathering of burghers recently, told them that he now recognised that their cause was quite hopeless and the prolong .ion of hostilities futile. Personally he would not taLe it upon himself to counsel surrender, but if to sur- render was inevitable it was folly to do so in two's and three's. The proper course was to come to a general agrpernent and to surrender as a nation.</p>
        <p>Contemplated Big Boer Surrender.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Burghers in Desperate Straits.</head>
        <p>Burghers in Desperate Straits.</p>
        <p>Fully 800 liners passed Pienaare River yesterday. moving in the direction of Nylstronm for the pur- pose, it is supposed, of discussing desertion from the fighting commandoes and ,surrender to the British. The Beers were in a deplorable state. Their clothing, was reduced to rags. Many were riding donkeys. Some were on foot, and all appesred to be in very great distress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Liverpool and Warrington. Regimental District. New Commandant.</head>
        <p>Liverpool and Warrington. Regimental District. New Commandant.</p>
        <p>The Man Who Saved General Hunter</p>
        <p>Colonel E. H. Fitzberbert, who has taken up the com- mand of the 8:h and 40th Regimental Districts, compris- ing the King's (Liverpool Regiment) and the .Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment), in suc- cession to Colonel E. J. H Spratt (retired), Is very highly esteemed in military circles as a soldier zealous for the service, and a gentleman. He began his service in the " King's Own " in 1865, and his almost magnetic per- sonality has always given him a wonderful degree of influence over those who have come under his nommand. Consideration for his men and all about him is a leading trait, but he is a thorough disciplinarian for all that. A man of high ideals both in soldiering and citizenship, he worthily upheld upon all occasions, the name of his old regiment, which in the present war haS been so ably represented by Major-General Sir Archibald Hunter, (formerly his subaltern), Major-General Sir Edward P Woodgate, and Colonel King, who commanded the Kimberley Mounted Corps that, with Colonel Mahon effected the relief of Mafeking. Colonel Fitzherbert enjoyed what must be considered as fairly rapid promo- tion,getting his majority three years after his captaincy. He wasmade lieutenant-colonel July 12, 1893, and full colonel, July 12, 1897. But for Colonel Fitzherbert, Hunter would have made no midnight sortiesfrorn Lady- smith to destroy the guns that threatened to raze the place, nor would he have captured Prinsloo and his host. After a trying time with yellow fever in the West Indies, when the itegtment buried six medical officers in three months, Hunter, vihose cheeriness and visitations among the stricken soldiers at all hours of the day and night had been " worth more than all the doctors," fell ill as the remnant of the regi- ment hurriedly quitted the plague-stricken shore. To die was Hunter's only wish as he lay agonising in the foul cabin, But assiduously and incessantly, Fitzher- bert nursed laim,and restored a gallant soldier to the service of his country,to bring laurels to the fame of his name and regiment." There is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother," and that is the essence of the friendship between Hunter and Fitzherbert. Colonel Fitz- herbert for 139 MP time held important commands at Kurrachee and Deolali. When Colonel Woodgate was appointed to raise native troops in West Africa, Colonel Fit zherbert took up the command of the regimental dis- trict at Lancaster. Last year he held temporarily, the command of the Hounslow distrie, while Colonel Guyon was commanding a brigade at Aldershot. Colonel Fitz herbert is a very forceful speaker, and the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families Association possesses no more ardent or effective advocate. He will see justice done to others but no power on earth would induce him to talk of him- self.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FREE VETERINARY BOOK.</head>
        <p>A FREE VETERINARY BOOK.</p>
        <p>We have just been informed that the Proprietors of Condy's Fluid, of 14, Goswell-road, L9ndon, E.C., are posting to all applicants, free of charge, a most useful Book containing instructions from Veterinary Surgeons of the highest standing for the treatment and cure of ailments and complaints affecting Horses, Dogs, Cats, Cattle, Sheep, Pigs, Rabbits, Poultry, Pigeons, Cage Birds, Gold Fish, &amp;c. Condy's Fluid is a really Magical Remedy for suffering animals and has had an enormous sale as a Veterinary remedy for nearly half a century. We recommend all our readers to write for this Veterinary Book to " Condy," 14, Goswell-road, E.C. NI charge for Book.</p>
        <p>Sad Drowning Fatality at Stockton Heath. A sad drowning fatality was reported to the police on Thursday. It appears that at 5,40 am. Thomas Price, aged 52, a blacksmith, who resided at 1, Bostock-street, Stockton Heath, and was em- ployed at Mr Houghton's smithy, Latchford was proceeding aloug the towing path of the Bridge- water Canal to his work. A dense fog prevailed at the time, and the unfortunate man missed his footing and fell into the canal at Twenty Steps Lock. A watchman named Pearse heard his cries for help, but was unable to render assistance. He raised an alarm and dragging operations were commenced, but the deceased's body was not recovered until half-past nine.</p>
        <p>BLINDNBSS. BYBSIGHT RBSTOItiID. --DISEASES OF THU YR CURSE) without operation. Book with full par- tteulare, free byp9st on mentioninz this paper.-- Address W. MELLOR, Eye speolallst. 238.003 rd road, Manhester. Prize Distribution at the Blue Coat School.</p>
        <p>An event which is always looked forward to v;i-t1; feelings of pleasure by the scholars at the Blue Coat School—the annual distribution of prizes— took place yesterday week. The Rev EC E Carle- ton, one of the trustees, presided, and there were also present —Mrs Carleton, Rev R. Greenall and Miss Greenall, Mrs Pickmere, the Misses White,Rev R Wild. Mr and Mrs Forstaaw, Miss Gaskell, Mr and Mrs Rose, Miss Rose. Mr and Mrs F Rose, and a large number of "Old Blues" and others interested in the institution. Letters were received from Mr Parr, Mr Roger Parr, Canon Penrhyn, Mr R Pierpoint, M.P., Dr. White, Mr James Fairclough, Mr Dakin, and the , Misses Litton, regretting their inability to be present.—A. musical programme was gone through in a manner which reflected the highest credit upon the performers, It included anthems, solos and choruses' recitations, duets,quartets, selections by the band, dumb-bell exercises, and a euphonium solo.—The following is a list of prize-winners:— Girls.— For general proftciency and good conduct: Bessie Ingham, Ellen Broadhurst, Dinah Smelling, 1 Louisa Matthews, Clara Smith, Emma Smith, Margaret Gerrard, Lucy bolt, Annie Hoult, Ellen Wood, Crams. Hardman. For good conduct (given by Mrs Litton) : Sarah E liamersley. For general superiority in all school mork (given by Mrs Pickmere) : Lily Snelling. For the beg essay on Christmas Time at the Blue Coat School : Mary Smith. Boys.—For general proficiency and good conduct: Edward Skelland, Thomas Rowley. John S Burgess, Walter Lawless, John R Galloway, George B Smallwood, Thomas Burgess, William EaStment, Frank 'tanner, Albert Maddock, I Reginald Essenhigh, John' Edwards, Harold Millett, William Lomax. John L Millett. For good conduct (given by Mrs Litton) : Thomas Rollins. General superiority in school work (given by Mrs Pickmere) : Waliam Sutch. Best essay on the war in South Africa: John Matthews.—The Rev E C E Carletsn, in the course of his remarks, referred to the very long connection which had existed between the Blue Coat School and Trinity Church,and gave an Ir.:count of the foundation of this and other charities which came into existence about the same period.—At the close of the pro- ceedings votes of thanks were proposed by the Rev Greenall, and seconded by Mr Forshaw. to Mr Carleton for presiding, and to Mr and Mrs Rose for the admirable manner in which the children had acquitted themselves.</p>
