      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SIR ROBERT BALL ON "TIME AND TIDE.'?</head>
        <p>SIR ROBERT BALL ON "TIME AND TIDE.'?</p>
        <p>Pioneer Lecture at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The second of the series of lectures promoted by the Newton-irellakerfield Lectures Committee was given on , Wednesday evening in the Town Hall, Earlestown, by ' Sir Robert Ball, LeL D., D.Sc., F.C.S. Mr Robert Stone presided, and there was a crowded audience. Sir Robert Ball in proceeding with his lecture. said:— I It was his pleasant task to try to tell a story of the good old times. The period was earlier than the reign of Queen Elizabeth. It was earlier than the Norman con- quest, it was even earlier than the time when our fore fathers roamed as savages through the ancient forests I of Britain. The time of his story was in fact earlier than any historical record, it was long before the first foot- I print of any human being had ever been made upen this earth. But man himself was only the creature of to-day. The date of hi story related to a time dimly discerned through the vista of a myriad centuries before his advent. En bad to tell of periods more ancient than those ages during which our globe was dominated by uncouth reptiles of gigantic bulk- an astonishing crew who, with all their kith and kin had long since utterly vanished. Our period was earlier than those singular ages of earth's history when flourished that beneficial vegetation which had been entombed as coal. Indeed at the dete of his story the profound mystery which we know as Life had not yet begun to move over the face of yesterday watersn i comparisonT h e beginning w ithofthlief life was antiquityitself a He would pass at one bound over thousands of eras each one of them far longer than all English history. There was a time when this solid earth of ours was not the hard cold body on which we now stand. The earth was than a globe of dazzling lustre glowing with fervent heat. Let us give this globe its proper designation. Twheehesavrst earth swcai ps tatehreend as luio Inc hi an pi at te7oet stuhne'm oßsyt sancientuch ibs ourryg In the universe. It narrates how our earth brought forth her first and only child, and how that child developed into the Silvery moon. No romance ever told a more wondrous tale than that which was told by" Time and Tide." It load been known from the very earliest times that the moon and the tides were in some way connected. A great advance had however to be made in was s thoughtf far re knowledge before it became possible to understand the true relation between the ebb and flow of theses, and the fhroamd read dngo t o f a a n people obvious who o chfaeritacgt oesir, movements of our silvery satellite. Indeed that relation doubt that tia; to whether the moon was the cause of the tides, or the tides the cause of the moon, The incessant scour of the tide on the shores of every ocean, acted in the same manner as does the air on the humming top. If we touch we check its speed.so that the constant tench of the tides is gradually checking the speed with which the earth is turning round. The tides never cease • year in and year out the great pulse of ocean throbs, and the effect of this everlasting rubbing is to make the earth turn round ever, more and more slowly. To put this conclusion into very brief language, we may Say that " The tides are increasing the length of the day." The day is always lengthening. To day is longer then yes- terday, to-morrow will be longer than to day. Viewed by our standard the (-flange is not a great one, it is toe small to be appreciable even by our most delicate obser- vations. In one thousand years the alteration in the length of a day is elliY a small fraction of a second ; but what may be a very small matter in one thousand years can become a very large one in many millions of 'years. Thug it is that when. we stretch our view through immense vistas of time past, or' when we look forward through immeasurable ages of time to come, the alteration in the length of the day is of the most startling proportions, and involves the most momentous consequences. There was a time when the day instead of being the 24 hours we now know, must have been only 23 hours. How many millions of years ago that was he did not pretend. to say, nor was the point material to the argument Suffice it to say, that assuming as geology assures us we may assume, the existence of these aeons of millions of veare, there was once a time when the day was not only one hour shorter, hot was even several hours less than it is at present. We need not stop our retrospect at a day of even twenty, or fifteen or ten hours long. We shall at once project our glance back to an im- wieasurstilv remote epoch, at which the earth was spin- ning round in a time only a quarter of the length of the present day. Thus have we arrived at the first stage in the wonderful Story of time and tide which has been mainly bresuzlit to light be the researceee of Professor G. H. D ervren. We hove seen how the tides on this earth have gradually lengthened the day from sex hours to the full four and twenty hours we now experience. A like effect has been produced in the moon. Originally the moon turned round on its axis very quickly •, it took no longer than Six hpure. The vehement tides which surged back- wards and forwards over its surface had the effect of lengthening the moon's period. This cause was indeed an much more energetic, that the moon's time of turning round was rapidly lengthened ; from being sir hours it increased to ten hours, tsventv hours, even to a whole day, but still this energetic tidal actin went on checking the moon's pace. Owing bottles small size of our satellite it had not the same capacity for resisting that action which our earth posseeses. The period of the moon's turning round on its axis increased to two days, to a week, a fortnight, three weeks, and at last to a month. When this point was reached the tides had done their work. Once they had sunceeded in slowing down the pace of the moon until our satellite revolved on its axis in just the same time as it takes to complete one eircnit around the earth. Then all that the tides rook] do had hems done. For all future time the sole duty of the tides would be to main- tain the rotation of the moon in exactly the same manner Once the speed of the moon had been SO adjusted that our satellite turned round on its axis in precisely the same time as it revolved round the earth, then a most striking result was arrived at. The same face of the moon was ever turned towards the earth. Looking back through the mists of time we see the moon ever drawing nearer and nearer to the earth. Our satellite new revolves at a distance of two hundred and forty thousand mileg, het there was a time when that distance was no more than two hundred thousand miles. There was a time when the Moon was but one hundred thousand miles away. As we look hack still earlier and earlier, we see the moon ever drawing Closer and closer to our globe, till at last we discern that the moon. instead of revolving where we new find it, was in feline. dilate proximity to the earth. Earlier still the moon mill have been actually touching the earth, On last protracted glimpse through the corridors of timeishow us how our satellite and the earth like a fnothell and a tennis hall attached tneether. are each revolving around the other in a period of e'sr hours. The earth has always been losing its heat. Therefore it was hotter twentr years ago than it was ten million years ago. We see in our reiwoenect the temperature ever hotter and hotter.' We find the thin shell of stolid rock no the surface is wax- ing thinner and thinner until at last our vision, stretched through the vista of illimitable centuries discloses our meth enee see in a solid creeeebut glowing over its entire amine with the dszzling brilliance of Molten metal There was a time in fact, when the whole earth was liquefied by heat, a heat so intense that the meet refractory granite, or rock would have to yield and become fluid. Such was the condition of our globe at that time of such critical interest, when the moon was quite close to it. We bad had a glimpse through the corridors of time by considering the history of the evelutien of the moms' He wished now, to give a *limps* throueh the corridors of time in the way whieh has been pointed nut by the geologists. It may employers the Antiquity of the period .they had to consider if they reflentee that with referenne to it, even the Persnaide of Egypt were but objects of to-day. No doubt the orectinn of that werpirrevs edifice was lost in the mists of enriellitv, but yet the earth in the days of the building of the Pyramids was much the SSW as our earth was now. Gm *mists would tell us that the few thooaandm of years, though they be half a dozen thousands which had elapsed since the last etnne was iaid on the greet pyramid, wag absolutely insignifiesue in comparison with the lapse nt geological time. Some years ago when he way in America, he visited Prefesser Marsh. the distingnished geologist in Yale College. The illnstrious geologist was then pile:wee in meting un the skeleton of a weederful fossil animal which he had discovered in the reeky moueteins of Colorado. This creature was indeed of eels's,' proportion's. For two years Professor Marsh, and a large staff of men had been engaged in carefully exhuming the bones of the reptile which was mere than one hundred feet long, and must have been at least 30 feet high. In recognition of the fact that this was the mast eplendid four footed beast which had ever graced this' earttetlereh called it the Atlantosaurne. What a vision of tentiquly did Such a creature as that bring before their eves. It was lint merely that hundreds of thoneande of years, or perhaps he might say milliene of years, had elapsed since that great reptile was hatched from the colossal egg which Ms neither had laid. It had been calculated that to compile such a thlekness of stratified cocks as that which our earth actually nessesve, a period of not less than two hundred and fifty million years must have elapsed. Therefore, urged the geologists, at the conclusion of this argument, the earth must have been fitted for life two hundred ane fifty million rears ago. It had been the main burden of his lecture te show that millions of years ago the moon arm much nearer to the earth than it new is. The distance of our 'satellite was certainly much less in the days when the F ezeon lived than at the present ' hour. He had no means of knowing how much nearer the moon was to lie when life was OW a new thing, but If this' distance bad been only 200,000 miles, instead of 240,000 as it now is, the difference in the tides would have had a startling effect. The daily rise and fall of the water would then have been double what they are at present. A rise arid fall to double the extent of our present tides would reach much farther up the rivers, the tide would periodically inundate a vastly increased area of lane. The moon and the earth had their origin by fire. nenlotiesa whee the moon Was horn it was AS hot as the mother earth which gave it birth. The moon and the earth have been cooling ever since.</p>
        <p>The moon being so small has cooled so completely that its internal fires have expired and its Crystallising minerals have drunk every drop of its oceans dry. The earth, the great body, has cooled to a certain extent,but ' not nearly en much as the moon ; it still retains a large share of its original heat ; it is still able to generate the eruptions of Vesuvius. The vast quantity of minerals which our globe clutainP, were stii so iargely in tile highly heated form that they had not yet desired to pass into the crystalline state. Their thirst for water had consequently not yet arisen to anything like the same extent as it was destined to do in the future ages when our globe shall have cooled as loss the moon, and when the thirst of the minerals will not have been slaked even though the last drop of the deepest ocean had been drained. All the volcanoes on the moon were now extinct; most of them were extinct on the earth. Owing, however, to the eize of the earth, it had still preserved some of its heat. It was therefore sometimes able to give us an eruption, and to show there was life in the old earth yet. (Applause.) Dr Handyside, in m oving a vote of thanks to the lecturer, said they were always delighted to see Sir Robert Ball, and he hoped they should not be long before they saw and heard him there again. (Hear, hear). Mr Collingwood seconded, and the vote was accorded with acclammation. Sir Robert Ball was much obliged for the kind way in which they accepted the proposal made. It was a great pleasure to him to be with them once again and to speak to them on a subject in which he was much interested. His task that night had bsen greatly assisted by the way in which the lantern had been worked. lie proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman, which was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>—.No__ COLONEL PILKINGTON AND TEMPERANCE LEGISLATION.</head>
        <p>—.No__ COLONEL PILKINGTON AND TEMPERANCE LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>---- ' Speaking in the House of Commons on Wednesday, 1 during the debate on Mr. Whittaker's amendment depre- cating the omission from the Speech from the Throne of any indication that adequate temperance legislation was likely to be introduced by the Government, Colonel Tilkington, whilst recognising that there was an open door in the matter of licensing reform thought the door was not very far open. (Laughter, and " Hear, hear.") He confessed to being much disappnintad, as were a great number of members on the same side of the House as hitnself, with the words in His Majesty's Speech in reference to the subject. because in their opinion the time was now ripe for a reform of the licensing laws. (Hear, hear.) The bon member for Lanarkshire (Dr. Douglas) had complimented hon. gentlemen on the Ministerial side of the House upon what he called their subserviency to their leaders in discipline and support, but there was a strong feeling on the Ministerial side of the House—. (hear,hear)—and although discipline might mean almost everything, so far as they were concerned, still to dis- appoint enthusiastic and faithful followers did not seem to him to be a true policy. The licensing laws were in confusion, and licensing authorities all over the country were met with the utmost difficulty in discharging their duties in the adminis- tration of those laws, (Hear, hear.) He did not care about the question of compensation at the present time, but what he did mostly care about was first of all that licensing authorities should have full power over licens- ing matters and over licensed premises wherever alco- holic liquor was sold. (Fl .ar, hear.) The hon member proceeded to give instances of anomalies that the chair- man of a licensing authority had to face in the discharge of his duty. He held t 3 the opinion that every licence should be under the same authority on the same conditions. One point he desired to see the Government introduce into their Bill, and on which they ought to settle legislation this session, was the position of all licences under the licensing authority. So far as the magistrates were concerned, he was willing that the authority should be conducted in a way that would enable them to deal properly with the licence. Further than that point there were many reforms that ought to be taken in hand by the Government when they came to deal with the question. He did not believe the Govern- enfant would live a better chance than the present for giving to the country a very good instalment of licensing reform. Everyone was ready for it, and it only wanted to be put into a Bill to pass. He hoped the idea of a mere Police Bill or any similar thing would be done away with altogether, and that the placing in position of the licensing authority, with proper and reasonable rights fer removing licensing anomalies, would be the first part of the Government Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>All Saints' MiSSion. The annual tea party war; held at All Saints' Miasion, on Monday evening. A lame number sat down to an excellent tea; after which the company thoroughly appreciated a eapital entertainment which consisted of dialogues, recitations, gramaphone, music and dancing. The Vicar, the Rev E R Tarbuck, presided. After a few remarks he proposed a vote of thanks, which was heart- ily accorded, to all who had in any way contributed to making the evening's entertainment successful. The pro- ceeiings terminated at 12 o'clock by the singing of " God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>St. Barnabas' Church.</head>
        <p>St. Barnabas' Church.</p>
        <p>The annual tea men g in connection with the above church took place in the schools, Thstock-street, on Monday evening, and was attended with success. Nearly 300 persons sat down to an excellent tea, lady members of the congregation presiding at the tables. After tea, the room was cleared and dancing was in- dnlged in with zest until midnight, to the strains of Mr Woodcock's band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>I There was an average attendance at the meeting of this Society on Sunday afternoon, when the chair was taken by Mr A Tilling, who delivered a brief address. The speaker for the afternoon was Mr G Shaw, of Man- chester. Miss Rothwell gave an efficient internretation of the two solos " The Promised Land "and "Sun of my Soul.' The meeting was also taken part in by Mr Healey and Mr S Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>St. Ann's Church—Congregational</head>
        <p>St. Ann's Church—Congregational</p>
        <p>Gathering.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational gathering in connection with St Ann's Church passed off very pleasantly on Monday evening. About 300 persons sat down to a capital tea in the schools, Dannett-street, lady members of the congregation presiding at the 'Abler After tea, the usual entertainment was held, the Vicar (Rev .1 Davies) occupying the chair. The first item was a rhosus "Hunting Song" which was spiritedly rendered by the choir, Mr E A Pachnore accompany- ing. A violin and piano duet "Marie," by Miss Hewitt. and Master Egerton was well received, and the round "Three blind mice" by the choir boys created much amusement. The remainder of till entertainment consisted of a sketch "The District School," which provoked a deal of mirth. The per- formers had all learnt their parts to perfection, and sus- tained the roles allotted to them in a manner which left r nothing to be desired. One of the " pupils "contributed a topical song " when you come to work it out," and there was not a dull moment from start to finish of the sk•tch--In proposing a hearty vote of thanks to the artistes and all who had in any way assisted the Vicar referred to the urgent necessity for new day schools in the parish. Plans had already been submitted to the Educational Department and approved, and the sum requ,red to erectrthe building was £3,000. The present accommodation was quite inaclequate,and many children had to be turned away.—The vote of thanks was heartily accorded and the proceedings terminated with the singing of" God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>..,.-.-...-...,.-...........</p>
        <p>THE " SANITAS " COMPANY, LIMITED.—The Annual General Meeting of this Company WAS held on Wednes- day, the 20th February, the Chairman Mr C T Kingzett, F.1.C., presiding. The total sales in the year under review, well exceeded those for the previous year, and the American branch also showed a satisfactory increase in sales, and contributed to the profits of the Company. The balance available for appropriation, after making provision for bad and doubtful debts, all repairs to plant and machinery, legal and other charges, wan sufficient to allow of a distribution (including the interim dividend paid in September last) of 71 per cent. The sum of £2,000 was placed to reserve and depreciation accounts, and £2,016 19; 91 carried forward. During the past year the Company's mew (additional) freehold factory at Limehouse had been more fully equipped, and Several new articles bad been added to their list of manufactures, including the "Formic Air-Purifier", and the " Formic Fumigator " appliencen for aerial disinfection and fumigation by means of Formaldehyde (a chemical agent now held in favour by many medical authorities). The Chairman thought that as the largest Disinfectant Manufacturers in the world, thy had well maintained their position, and pointed out that it was their object and determination to keep well abreast with the progress of chemical science in its application to the subject of disinfection. tie was also able to state that there had been a satisfactory increase in the sales of the popular" Sanitas " Fluid and Powder, and expressed a strong conviction that as in the past twenty-two years during which the business haddeeen in existence, so in the future, under careful guidance and scientific con- trol, it would continue to grow and prosper ; for althounh it was not concerned with the material wants of the public, it skilfully ministered to their sanitary requirements and personal health.</p>
        <p>DELICIOUS MAZAWATTHE TEA DELICIOUS MAZkWATTEN TEA DELICIOUS M4,ZA.WATTEN SOLE AGENTS : HENRY MILLING AND CO.. LTD WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC.. 5, HORSEMARXET-STREET, WARRIN4TON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalistl Though it did not rain hot water as suggested in this column,the thaw on Friday evening and Saturday morn- ing was so rapid that the Wilderspool ground became fit for play early in the afternoon. The telegraph acquainted Broughton Ringers of the fact and they hastily scraped a team together for the purpose of doing battle with Warrington. They were so surprised at the ultimatum or intimation—call it what you like— that they came with only 14 men arid played the ga-ne one man short all through. Natur- ally fourteen men were scarcely a match for Warrington's fifteen and the latter had an easy thing on apparently Broughton', fourteen were a doughty lot however, and they came so near beating Warring- ton that one almost shudders yet to think of it. The full score was 2 points to nil in favour of Warrington— not a very great and glorious victory, eh ?</p>
        <p>It was a decided relief to the crowd to find the game playable, for the thaw had come with such suddenness and determination that skating was very much •• oph " and the probability appeared to be that they would be between the devil and the deep sea (metaphorically speaking) during the whole of Saturday afterno3n. But as I have related, things got so sultry down at Wilderspool that the turf softened at lightning Speed, and the match 'wad: brought to a conclusion. In addition to coming short in members the Rangers had to find substitutes for W James and Hogg, two of the best backs, and their ab- sence undoubtedly seriously crippled the team. War— rington must be accounted very fortunate on Satur- ' day.</p>
        <p>The opening stages of the game fostered the impres- sion that the visitors were going to be made mincemeat of, for Warrington overplayed them at every point, not- withstanding that wind and: sun were against:them. Strong forward work and smartness behind kept the visitors continually on the defensive, and it looked odds on a try or two accruing to the home lot before they changed ends. Alt they did get was a goal from the foot of Fish, which proved to be the winning point. Probably if anybody had taken a census of opinion round the ground he would not have found a dont" people who believed at half-time that the score would remain unaltered. Warrington had played so well against unfavourable conditions that it seemed certain they must make things hum when they got the wind behind them. There was one individual situated near the press box who posed as a prophet, however, and unlike prophets generally he guessed—beg pardon—prophesied aright. lEle stuck to it that two points to nil would be the score at the finish, and though I put him down in my note book as a faol of the first water he came out smiling at the end.</p>
        <p>Somehow or other the wind appeared of no use to the home team and the sun had,of course, compassionately gone in. The breeze quite refreshed the Rangers and they started bustling Warrington about in a manner the reverse of polite. According to the theory ot the game they ought to have dust laid themselves hown and died in the second half, instead of which they behaved very rudely and warly scored a try at Warring- ton's expense. There was actually nothing to choose between the teams this half, and the occasions were unpleasantly frequent when the fourteen Rangers got the better of the fifteen wirepullers and became the attacking party. Their vigorous efforts were in vain, sad to say, and Warrineton carried off the points at the close by the narrowest margin possible when only one side scores.</p>
        <p>Probably there were ructions at the next committee meeting of the Broughton Club following the match. First class teams do not fulfil engagements a man short at all often, and the novelty, with its results, will not be appreciated by the Rangers' executive, especially as they bad fifteen "A" team players doing nothing. Eiere is another instance of Warrington's luck, for they are not bound to fulfil the" A" team fixture and thus pocket the £lO which the expenses of the match would have come to.</p>
        <p>On the Warrington side, Isherwood was undoubtedly theibest of a set of backs who did themselves justice, though they were deficient in scoring power. He played splendidly and during the first half made dashing runs which he has not equalled this season, In fact the immense amount of work he did told on him and he tired perceptibly towards the close. The fact that he had two powerful speedy centres against him had something to do with this. Burgess showed an improvement on his recent play, and passed the ball with far greater accur- acy and speed than is usual. The forwards did well at the commencement, but were either overplayed or lazy in the second half. They ought to have easily held the seven opposed to them. Broughton owed much of their success in keeping the score down to the speed and safety of their backs. They were faster than the War rington men and combined in a fashion that took a lot of ! beatific,: Wilson has come on tremendously and is a most crashing, resourceful three-quarter. He does not seem like the same man whom we used to meet at More- cambe. W James was much missed at half, his brother Sam and All Barratt being a very ill-assorted pair.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, Warrington tackled Oidbam at Oldham, and got the usual drubbing, though the state of the ground was not favourable to good football. The weather seems to have been of a much severer character at Watersbeddings than here, and there was plenty of snow and slush in the neighbourhood, while the ground was like a small swamp. It had been plentifully covered with straw, but the successive frosts and thaws made the surface very sticky. There was not enough grass on the whole field to feed a naz, and the greasy substitute left its mark when anybody fell.</p>
        <p>Both teams were short of three regular players, but in the Oldham case the suffering in consequence was very slight. Good substitutes were plentiful, while Warrington were so hard up that Radcliffe played full back, a position entirely strange to him. Burgess was another absentee, and he is, I understand, an inmate of Mr Allison's establishment at Manchester suffering from a damaged knee. " Smack " Bate filled the vacany, and the Warrington team reminded one of old times with two Bates behind the scrimmage.</p>
        <p>A description of the match is not difficult. 'Oldham adapted themselves very well to the muddy conditions and were smarter in every department than Warrington. Early on, Civil, one of Oldham's recent recruits, got hold at centre and made the Warrington defence look very feeble by scoring behind the posts. A goal followed, and as the irrepressible Sam Williams also got in before half•time, Warrington were in a minority of 8 points when they crossed over. The second half yielded 2 tries to Oldham, Fletcher being the scorer in both cases., so that the total reached WO, It has been said of late that Fletcher was " done," but</p>
        <p>If he can play wing regularly as well as on Tuesday be would be a valuable acquisition-to most clubs. Civil is another man likely to make his mark, as he is just the build for centre play. All the Oldham backs did good work, and especial praise ig due to Dick Thomas, the redoutable full back, who played as cool and confident a game as ever I saw any man play. He was head and shoulders above young Radcliffe, who, however, cannot be blamed for that, At half too, Oldham were much the smarter, the two Bates appearing very slow in com- parison with A Lees and Lawton. Elockenhall was one of the best men en the Warrington side, doing excellent service on the defence from start to finish.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For Agst. P. W. L. D. G T. p. a T. P. P. Swinton 20...18... 1... 1...23 67-247...10 8— Oldham 20...17... 3... 0...25 68-254...12 10— 54...34 Runcorn 22...17... 5... 0...38 ?6-184...13 19— 83-31 Broughton R'ng'523...14... 7... 2...30 41-183-17 16— 82...30 Salford 21...11...10.,, o_2B 39_173...26 25-127...22 Leigh 21...10...10... 1...21 25-117...17 28-118...21 Warrington 23... 9...11... 3...18 24-108...18 28-120-21 Barrow 20... 9_10... 1...22 28-128...20 27-121-19 Rochdale Hornete2o... 6...12... 2...18 13— 75...30 49-267...14 Milton' 19... 6...13... 0...15 13— 69...23 30-136-12 *St. Helens 22... 6...14... 2...13 15— 71...30 41...183...12 Widnes. 20... 6-14... 0...16 13. 71...23 50-196...12 Stockport 22... 4...15_ 3...13 15— 71...27 37-165...11 Wigan 19... 4-12— 3...12 14— 66-25 43-181-11 *St. Helens have had two points deducted for a breach of the rules.</p>
        <p>Warrington's balance of p3intg i 3 novr on the wrong side, and the scoring powers of the team will have to be demonstrated very quickly if the lost ground is to bet regained at all. It seems probable that we shall go more points than last season as the rernainiog three matches are at home but the general effectiveness of the team is not near so great.</p>
        <p>Stockport visit the Warrington enclosure to-day and may expect a " walloping " in return for the one ad- ministered at Edgeley Park. Warrington are expecting to be fully reprostnted again, and I hope they will fully establish their superiority over the club which looks the likeliest candidate for the wooden spoon.</p>
        <p>--- The following matches in the Lancashire Senior Com- petition will belplayed to-day Leigh v Wigan. Millona v St. Beton-% Oldham v Runcorn Rochdale Hornets v Barrow. Swinton v Broughton Rangers. Warrington v Stockport. Widnes v Salford: (To be played on the ground of the first-named club in each case.</p>
        <p>FOOT RACING. Sankey Harriers are to be congratulated upon their creditable performance at the Cross Country Champion.</p>
