        <p>..... _ BIBLE ADDIVSSES ARE DELIVERED EVERY SUNDAY AT 6.30 P.M.. Ice TOFT'S ASSEMBLY ROOMS, ConNEn OF BRIDGE-STREET AND FRIARS'-GATE, WARRINGTON. SUBJECT FOR SUNDAY NEXT The Gospel. Who should preach it. WARRINGTON UNION. SEWING MISTRESS and ASSISTANT to MATRON. The Guardians of the above Union require the services of a capable woman to the post of Sewing Mistress. The officer appointed will bays charge of the female department in body of house (not children). Salary £25 per annum with uniform, rations, apartments, .tc. Fin ther information and list of duties can be obtained en application to the Matron. Applications. with Copies of three recent testimonials, to be sent to me On or before February 27th, 1901. Reasonable travelling expenses allowed. By Order, J 13 SUTTON. Clerk. reWSey Chambers, Warrington. February 13th 1931. - GREAT CENTRAL, RAILWAT.-114YDOCK PARK STE NPLECHARES, IsT AND 2ND MARCH. On above dates EXCURSION to HAYDOCK PARK (Race Course Station) will leave St. Helens (Central) at 1.25 p.m. .Maydock 1.32 p.m. each day. TIME or liETITult, see Bills. Ordinary First and Third 'Class, Single and Return Tickets will be available. If the bteeplechases are postponed, Trains will not run. WILLIAM POLLITT. General Manager. Manchester NEWTO N-IN-MAKELIFIELD, U.D.C.—GAS DEPARTMENT. COKE. COKE for SALE at the GAS WORKS, NEWTON-LE- WILLOWS in large or small quantities, delivered in bags at " any address within the District at iSs. per ton. Order forms may be obtained at the COLLECTOR'S 'OFFICE, Town Hall, Earlestown. or at the Gas Works, Purchasers not consuming Gas or consuming gas through Automatic Meters are requested to send cash with order. WARRINGTON UNION.—The Guardians are desirous of ALTERING the MODE of SELLING their CHOPPED-UP FIRS-WOOD. They, therefore, invite TENDERS from persons in the firewood trade to purchase same either in bundles or loose, in such quantities as may be produc,l each week; therefore they ask for tenders. The MastAr of the Workhouse is open to receive same, and place tbem before the Guardians at an early meeting. Febru ry 19th, 1901. TO THE ELECTOR OF Tar: APPLKTON DIVISION. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— The triennial election of members for the Cheshire County Council will be held early in Mann, and I venture •cnce more to offer myself as your representative for the Appleton Division. My best services have been and always Will be devoted to the interests of my constituency and to the County ganerally, and if you again honour me with your Confidence, I can only assure you that no efforts of mine Will be wanting to fulfil the duties of the position. I have the honour to be, Ladies and Gentlemen. Yours very faithfully. J. CHARLTON PARR. Grappenball Heyes, ,bruary 19th, 1901. _</p>
        <p>p E AEL LIFE AF4:AI KAMM COyIPANY, LIMITED. . ANNUAL REPORT FOR THE YEAR ENDING DECEMBER 318 T, 1900, Presented at the Thirty-Seventh Annual General Meeting, iheld at the Offices of the Company. London Bridge, City, 8.C., FRIDAY. FEBRUARY 15th, 1901. The Report and Statement of Accounts for the year, whfch the Directors now present, bear evilence that the work of termer years was of a very solid character. and although many events have nccured. tending to retard and unsettle business operations during the past year, it is a pleasure to 'bow that the results again outstrip anything hitherto achieved by the Company. The rate of progress can be readily seen by the following e°l34parison between the Company's position now, and at the land of the previous qainqueunium. PREMIUM INCOME. ASSETS. 1895—£422,894 £510.978 1970—£841,743 21,200,286 !the Premium Income has been nearly doubled, and the Assets considerably more than doubled during the past quinquennium. tn the Ordinary Branch the Premium Inoome for 1900 -'4eunted to £100,780, being an increase of £21,093 over that f the Previous year. The Premiums on New Policies issued nUrlng the year amount to £33,643 165., assuring the sum of R,887.060 The Quinquennial Valuation which has just been completed by R M Moore. Req.. A.1.A.., up to December, 1930, shows a surplus of £203,588. The Directors recommend that the sum of £30,993 be dis- tributed among the Participating Policyholders and Share- Widens, and that the balance of £172,595 be carried forward. INCOME. The total Income for the year amounted to £882,622 17s 6d being an increase of £118,360 21 SS over that of last year. PREMIUMS (DECEMBER 1901) The premium Income on the Policies in force on the Coas- tal:Ws books, including 871.562 new Polices issued during he year amounts to £952,865 15s 11d. CLAIMS. ~,, The Company has paid 48,421 Life Claims and Matured Tadowmente during the year, amounting with Bonuses, to w.-4UU7O 12s sd, which, added to the sums previously paid in eielaas since the establishment of the Company, make a total Of £2,747,192 lie Bd. - ACCUMULATED FUNDS. The Assurance Funds, with Capital paid up,now amount to £1.2..^0.C86 as Id., showing an increase for the year of £201,973 17s. 3d. By Order, P. J. FOLEY, Managing Director. See Reports and Opinions a the Press as to the remarkable Progress made by the Company. Wanted, additional repre- sentatives in all districts. To good business men liberal terms and certain success.—Apply to the Managing Director at the Chief Office. Adelaide Buil tinge, Londmi Bridge, City E. 0., or the District Superintendent. MR. JOSEPH M &amp;HINSON. 5, Victoria-road, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>(.01LIN k utiEs3TER. WILD BIRDS PROTECTION ACTS, 1880, 1894. and 1896. NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN, that by an Order made by the Secretary of State, pursuant to the above Acts, it is provided inter alia as follows. viz. :- 2. The time during which the killing and taking of 'wild birds, or any of them, is prohibited by the Wild Birds Protection Act, 1880 (43 and 44 Vic„ cap 35), shall be extended in the County of Chester so as to be from the Ist day of March to the 12th day of August in each year. 3. The Act of 1880, as extended by Section 2of this Order, shall apply within the whole of the County of Chester to the Kestrel and Heron as if those species Were included in the Schedule of that Act. 4. The taking or killing of the following species of birds, namely Fisher, (Neat Crested Grebe, Ileum, Goldfinch, and Owl, shall be prohibited Within the whole of the County of Chester during the whole of that period of the year to which the protection of wild birds under the Act of 1880 (as extended by Section 2 of this Order) does not extend. 5. The taking or destroying of the eggs of the Owl, the Bittern, the King Fisher, the Kestrel, the Heron, the laeldrake, the Dunlin, the Blackheaded Gull, the Com- lhan Tern, and the Oyster Catcher, shall be prohibited in the whole County of Chester 6. The taking or killing of the Gull and the Tern is trohlpited within the following parishes in the Admino- rative Cm-ity of Chester during the whole of that Deriod of the year to which the protection of wild birds !Under the Wild Birds Protection Act, 1880 (as extended by Section 2 of this Order), does not extend, viz. : PUDDINGTON WALLASEY BURTON LOWER BEBINGTON NESS BROMBOROUGH NESTON-CUM-PARK. E kSTIIAM GATE INCE HOOTON GREAT STANNEY NETHERPOOL FRODSHAM LORD- WHITBY SHIP STANLOW W ESTON GAYTON RUNCORN lIESWALL-CUM- H ALTON OLDFIELD NORTON THURSTASTON MOORE CALDY ACTON GRANGE HOYLAITE-CUM. WALTON INFERIOR , WEST KIRBY Tcluding therein so much of the estuaries of the Rivers "oe and Mersey as lies within the administrative County of Chester. PENALTIES. Any person acting in contravention of the above- mentioned Order renders himself liable to the Penalties Mentioned in the said Acts. REGINALD POTTS. Clerk of the Cheshire Couaty Council tth February, l9Ol.</p>
